(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\).  \n   The set of these points is collinear with slope \\(-1\\) after a suitable affine transformation; consequently there exists a sunny line (with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) passing through any two of them, and because the points are consecutive on the vertical line, the whole set is still covered by the new sunny line together with the unchanged horizontal and diagonal lines.\n\n   By performing this replacement for any subset of the vertical lines we obtain, for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\), a configuration containing exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The above replacement argument shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A base covering with only non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the following family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: x=i , &&\\text{for } i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\\\\\nL_{h}&: y=1 ,\\\\\nL_{d}&: x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non‑sunny (they are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-2\\)*, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is \\(L_a\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(b=1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_h\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the diagonal line \\(L_d\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with **zero** sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line\n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \nThe vertical line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet us choose any two distinct points of \\(P_i\\), say \\((i,b_1)\\) and \\((i,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1<b_2\\).  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{i-i}= \\infty ,\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is vertical. To obtain a **sunny** line we must change the slope.  \nObserve that the set \\(P_i\\) is an arithmetic progression in the \\(y\\)-coordinate; therefore for any real number \\(\\lambda\\neq 0\\) the line\n\n\\[\nS_{i,\\lambda}:\\; y-\\bigl(b_1+\\lambda\\bigr)=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{\\lambda}\\,(x-i)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npasses through the two points \\((i,b_1+\\lambda)\\) and \\((i,b_2-\\lambda)\\).  \nChoosing \\(\\lambda\\) small enough (e.g. \\(\\lambda= \\tfrac12\\)) guarantees that both points still belong to the integer lattice segment \\(P_i\\).  \nMoreover the slope of \\(S_{i,\\lambda}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{\\lambda}\\), which is finite and different from \\(-1\\) for a generic choice of \\(\\lambda\\). Hence \\(S_{i,\\lambda}\\) is sunny.\n\nBecause the points of \\(P_i\\) are consecutive on the vertical line, any point of \\(P_i\\) lies on the segment joining the two perturbed points used in (4); consequently it also lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i,\\lambda}\\).  \nThus we may **replace** the vertical line \\(L_i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{i,\\lambda}\\) without losing any coverage of points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) be any subset with \\(|J|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in J\\) replace the vertical line \\(L_i\\) by a sunny line \\(S_{i,\\lambda_i}\\) constructed as in §2 (choose the parameters \\(\\lambda_i\\) independently).  \nFor \\(i\\notin J\\) keep the original vertical line \\(L_i\\).  \n\nDenote the resulting family of lines by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nBy construction:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (the replacements are distinct because they have different slopes or different intercepts);\n* exactly the lines indexed by \\(J\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines;\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered, because the only lines that were altered still contain all the points they originally covered (see §2).\n\nSince \\(J\\) can be any subset of size \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have exhibited, for each such \\(k\\), a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n> (i) each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThe existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains unresolved. ∎"
