 Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+y=i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\), hence is non\u2011sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid i=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore a required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) consider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,1),\\qquad Q_t=(1,t),\\qquad R_t=(t+1,t).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t+1+t\\le n+1\\) (equivalent to \\(t\\le n-2\\)), all three belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThey are collinear on the line  \n\n\\[\nM_t:\\;y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x-\\frac{t}{t+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence each \\(M_t\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines \\(M_t\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nThe two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) (the extreme points of \\(T_n\\)) are covered by the vertical sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad V_n:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n=\\bigl\\{M_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-2\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{V_1,V_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n-2)+2=n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus a configuration exists for \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any intermediate number of sunny lines\n\nWe now show how to pass from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all non\u2011sunny) to \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) (all sunny) by a sequence of elementary replacements, each of which increases the number of sunny lines by exactly one while preserving the covering of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nConsider the three diagonals  \n\n\\[\nL_{t+1}:\\;x+y=t+1,\\qquad\nL_{2}   :\\;x+y=2,\\qquad\nL_{t+2}:\\;x+y=t+2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTheir intersection with \\(T_n\\) consists respectively of the single points  \n\n\\[\n(t,1),\\qquad (1,1),\\qquad (t+1,t),\n\\]\n\ntogether with the points \\((1,t)\\) (which also lies on \\(L_{t+1}\\)).  \nHence the set of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by the three lines in (2) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,1),(1,t),(t+1,t)\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy (1) these three points are collinear on the sunny line \\(M_t\\).  \nTherefore we may **replace** the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_{t+1},L_{2},L_{t+2}\\) by the single sunny line \\(M_t\\) **and** keep the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\); the total number of lines stays \\(n\\) and the covering of \\(T_n\\) is unchanged.  \nThe number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\nPerforming this replacement for distinct values \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,s\\) (with \\(s\\le n-2\\)) yields a configuration consisting of  \n\n* the sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_s\\);\n* the two untouched extreme diagonals \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{n+1}\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-2-s\\) diagonals \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\notin\\{2,\\,t+1,\\,t+2\\ (1\\le t\\le s)\\}\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is still \\(n\\); the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(s\\).  \nThus for every integer \\(s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with \\(k=s\\).\n\nTo obtain \\(k=n-1\\) we start from the all\u2011sunny configuration \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) and replace one of the two vertical lines, say \\(V_1\\), by the non\u2011sunny diagonal \\(L_{2}\\).  \nThe covering is preserved because both lines contain the point \\((1,1)\\).  \nNow the configuration has \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the already constructed cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) we have exhibited, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nSince the configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).  \nHence the list \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) is exhaustive.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]"
