1,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(D_2\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)* the argument is symmetric, using the vertical lines \\(V_a\\) and the diagonal \\(D_1\\) or \\(D_2\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nThus either \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\)) or \\(b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAmong the lines (2)–(4) exactly two are sunny, namely the two diagonal lines \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal), to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical) or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll lines \\(H_c\\) are horizontal, all lines \\(V_c\\) are vertical, and both \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\) have slope \\(-1\\) but are *not* parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) because they are distinct translates; a line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) only when it has the same equation \\(x+y=0\\).  \nHence \\(H_c\\) and \\(V_c\\) are non‑sunny, while \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\) are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the family (2)–(4) provides a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\nTo obtain configurations with fewer sunny lines we simply replace one or both of the diagonal lines by non‑sunny lines that still cover the uncovered points:\n\n* Replace \\(D_1\\) by the vertical line \\(V_1: x=1\\).  \n  The resulting family contains only the sunny line \\(D_2\\); therefore we have a configuration with \\(k=1\\).\n\n* Replace both \\(D_1\\) and \\(D_2\\) by the vertical line \\(V_1\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_1: y=1\\).  \n  All lines are now non‑sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=2\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound \\(k\\le 2\\)\n\nRecall that a **sunny** line has a slope different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can intersect each of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=b\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=a\\mid a\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nin at most one point; otherwise two distinct intersection points with the same family would force the line to be parallel to that family, contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\nTherefore a single sunny line can contain **at most three** points of the set \\(T_n\\) (one from each family (5)).  \n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nEach non‑sunny line belongs to one of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\); such a line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the whole row, column or diagonal).  \n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the whole configuration is at most  \n\n\\[\n3k+(n-k)(n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we must have  \n\n\\[\n3k+(n-k)(n-1)\\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (7) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \\frac{2}{n-5}\\qquad (n\\ge 6).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4,5\\) the inequality can be checked directly; in each case it forces \\(k\\le 2\\).  \nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, (7) implies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration satisfying the covering condition can contain more than two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown\n\n* the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are realizable by explicit constructions, and  \n* any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
