l\n   line \\(x=p\\) is removed from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and the sunny line\n   \\(S_{p-1}\\) is added, then every point of \\(T_n\\) that lost its unique\n   covering line is now covered by the added line.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   A point that loses its only covering line must have abscissa \\(a=p\\) and\n   ordinate \\(b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) (otherwise it would also lie on the\n   horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that remains in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\)).  The condition\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(p+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-p\\).  Because\n   \\(p\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) we have \\(n+1-p\\ge\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\), so the\n   interval \\([\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1,\\;n+1-p]\\) is non\u2011empty and contains at\n   least one integer.  All such points are of the form \\((p,b)\\) with\n   \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\le b\\le n+1-p\\).  By (3) the line \\(S_{p-1}\\)\n   contains exactly the points \\((a,a+p-1)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-p}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  In particular,\n   for \\(a=p\\) we obtain the point \\((p,2p-1)\\), and the inequalities above\n   guarantee that \\(2p-1\\le n+1-p\\); hence \\((p,2p-1)\\in T_n\\) and lies on\n   \\(S_{p-1}\\).  Every other point \\((p,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq2p-1\\) already lies\n   on a horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \u220e\n\n   The lemma shows that we may replace any vertical line \\(x=p\\) of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) by the sunny line \\(S_{p-1}\\) without destroying the\n   covering property.\n\n5. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f1.*  Delete from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the first \\(k\\) vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f2.*  For each deleted line \\(x=i\\) (with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) insert the\n   sunny line \\(S_{i-1}\\).\n\n   The resulting family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j