\((3,4)\\) is not covered. Therefore the lemma is false.\n\n*Consequences:* The subsequent construction that replaces vertical lines by sunny lines is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) (Step 5)\n> “Delete from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) the first \\(k\\) vertical lines … insert the sunny line \\(S_{i-1}\\) for each deleted line.”\n\n*Critical Error.* This step assumes the Replacement Lemma holds for every \\(i\\) up to \\(k\\). Since the lemma is false for many \\(i\\) (especially when \\(i\\) is large), the resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) may fail to cover all points of \\(T_n\\). For instance, with \\(n=6\\) and \\(k=3\\) the construction yields vertical lines \\(x=4\\) (which does not exist) and sunny lines \\(S_0, S_1, S_2\\); the point \\((3,4)\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the claim that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) works for all \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) (Step 6)\n> “The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each intersect the vertical line \\(x=p\\) in at most one point, so together they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of the column \\(x=p\\). The column \\(x=p\\) contains \\(n+1-p\\) points of \\(T_n\\). … since \\(n+1-p > n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\), the column cannot be fully covered – a contradiction.”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \n- The inequality \\(n+1-p > n-1\\) is **not** true for all \\(p\\). For \\(p=2\\) we have \\(n+1-p = n-1\\), equality, not strict inequality.  \n- More importantly, the argument ignores that the **non‑sunny** line itself (the vertical line \\(x=p\\)) is part of the covering and already covers *all* points of that column. The sunny lines add at most one extra point each, but the column does not need to be covered solely by sunny lines. Hence the contradiction does not follow, and the impossibility claim is unproved.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Conclusion\nThe solution’s final statement that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) is **not