ontain \\(s\\) sunny and \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can intersect\n   \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (it can be one side of the triangle).  \n\n   *If \\(t=0\\):* Lemma 1 gives \\(2s\\ge|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), i.e.\n   \\(s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}4>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), impossible.  \n\n   *If \\(t=1\\):* at most \\(n\\) points are covered by the unique non‑sunny line,\n   leaving \\(|T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points to be covered by sunny lines.\n   By Lemma 1 this needs at least \\(\\big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-1)}4\\big\\rceil\\) sunny\n   lines, so the total number of lines would be\n   \\(1+\\big\\lceil\\frac{n(n-1)}4\\big\\rceil>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\n   Hence any feasible configuration must have \\(t\\ge2\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k=s\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (1) is a **necessary condition**.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration (maximal sunny lines).*  \n   Take the two non‑sunny lines that form the legs of the right‑angled\n   triangle:\n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \n   The remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   I_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\},\n   \\qquad |I_n|=\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\((a,b)\\in I_n\\) draw the line through \\((a,b)\\) and the\n   fixed point \\((1,1)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   L_{a,b}:\\; \\text{through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(a,b).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\).  \n   Because \\(a,b\\ge2\\), this slope is a positive rational number different\n   from \\(0,\\,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_{a,b}\\) is sunny.\n   Choose **any** \\(n-2\\) distinct points of \\(I_n\\) and keep the corresponding\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   Together with \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) we obtain a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{\\,n-2}= \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{L_{a_i,b_i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   consisting of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering all points of\n   \\(T_n\\), and containing \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Reducing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{\\,n-2}\\) we can replace any sunny line\n   \\(L_{a,b}\\) by a non‑sunny diagonal line \\(D_{c}\\) of slope \\(-1\\) that\n   passes through the same point \\((a,b)\\) (take \\(c=a+b\\)).  \n   The diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) also contains the point \\((c-1,1)\\) (or \\((1,c-1)\\)),\n   which is already covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\); therefore no new uncovered\n   point is created.  The replacement reduces the sunny count by one while\n   keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n   By performing this replacement exactly \\((n-2)-k\\) times we obtain a\n   configuration \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Because we may replace any subset of the sunny lines, this works for **every**\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary, and the construction\n   above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\n   attainable.  Hence the admissible values are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
