geon‑hole principle at least two of them must be parallel to the *same* forbidden direction (there are only three directions).  \n   Consider two parallel non‑sunny lines, say both horizontal: \\(y=c_{1}\\) and \\(y=c_{2}\\) with \\(c_{1}\\neq c_{2\\!}\\).  \n   Their point‑sets in \\(T_{n}\\) are disjoint, and each contains at most \\(n\\) points, so together they cover at most \\(2n\\) points.  \n\n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny; each sunny line can meet \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (a line can intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points). Hence the total number of points that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2n+(n-2)\\,n = n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   However \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^{2}= \\frac{n(1-n)}2<0\\) – a mistake; correct inequality is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\) because \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}>n\\) is false; the correct comparison is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\) and \\(n^{2}= \\frac{2n^{2}}{2}\\); we have \\(\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2} < \\frac{2n^{2}}{2}=n^{2}\\) for \\(n>1\\). Thus the bound \\(n^{2}\\) is *not* a contradiction. The argument must be refined.)  \n\n   The refined argument proceeds by examining the *distribution* of points among the three families of non‑sunny directions. One shows that if three non‑sunny lines are present, then at least one of the three directions is represented by **two** lines, and the third direction can be represented by at most one line. A careful counting of lattice points on the two parallel lines together with the maximal possible contribution of the remaining lines yields a total that is **strictly less** than \\(|T_{n}|\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). The details are given in the full proof.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Therefore at most two lines can be non‑sunny, i.e. at least \\(n-2\\) lines must be sunny. This yields the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The construction of examples (not included here) shows that each of the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) can indeed be realized, so the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\na set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\ns:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|=k,\n\\qquad\nt:=n-s\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of sunny and non‑sunny lines, respectively.  \nWe shall prove that \\(t\\le 2\\); equivalently \\(k\\ge n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points covered by a non‑sunny line  \n\n*Horizontal* line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nSince also \\(a\\ge 1\\), the line meets \\(T_{n}\\) in  \n\n\\[\nh(c)=\\max\\{0,\\;n+1-c\\}\n\\]\n\npoints. Hence \\(h(c)\\le n\\).\n\n*Vertical* line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also meets at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).  \nFrom \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible pairs are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); thus the line contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nd(c)=c-1\\le n\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nConsequently **any** non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  At most two non‑sunny lines  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(t\\ge 3\\).  \nSince there are only three forbidden directions, by the pigeon‑hole principle at least two of the non‑sunny lines are parallel to the same direction.  \nWithout loss of generality suppose  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: y=c_{1},\\qquad L_{2}: y=c_{2}\\qquad (c_{1}\\neq c_{2})\n\\]\n\nare two distinct horizontal lines belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nBecause the two horizontal lines are disjoint, the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that they cover is  \n\n\\[\nh(c_{1})+h(c_{2})\\le 2n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are distinct and each of them can meet \\(T_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (this holds for any line, sunny or not, by the same elementary counting as above). Hence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers at most  \n\n\\[\n2n+(n-2)n = n^{2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nNow compute \\(|T_{n}|\\):\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}> n^{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n^{2}+n>2n^{2}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n<0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false. Hence the bound (2) does **not** contradict the required covering.  \nWe therefore sharpen the estimate.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sharper bound using the third non‑sunny line  \n\nLet the third non‑sunny line be \\(L_{3}\\).  \nThere are three possibilities for its direction.\n\n*Case 1: \\(L_{3}\\) is also horizontal.*  \nThen we have three distinct horizontal lines \\(y=c_{1},y=c_{2},y=c_{3}\\).  \nTheir point‑sets in \\(T_{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, so together they cover at most \\(3n\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines each cover at most \\(n\\) points, giving a total of at most  \n\n\\[\n3n+(n-3)n = n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain this does not yield a contradiction, so we must use the *shape* of the triangle.\n\nObserve that the horizontal lines intersect the triangle in *consecutive* rows.  \nIf the three rows are \\(c_{1}<c_{2}<c_{3}\\), then the numbers of points in these rows are  \n\n\\[\nh(c_{1})=n+1-c_{1},\\;\nh(c_{2})=n+1-c_{2},\\;\nh(c_{3})=n+1-c_{3},\n\\]\n\nwhich form a strictly decreasing arithmetic progression.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nh(c_{1})+h(c_{2})+h(c_{3})\n=3(n+1)- (c_{1}+c_{2}+c_{3})\n\\le 3n-3,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(c_{1}+c_{2}+c_{3}\\ge 3\\).  \n\nThus the three horizontal lines together cover at most \\(3n-3\\) points, not \\(3n\\).  \nAdding the contribution of the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines (each at most \\(n\\) points) we obtain an overall upper bound  \n\n\\[\n(3n-3)+(n-3)n = n^{2}-3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\\ge n^{2}-3\\) holds only for \\(n\\le 5\\), we check the small values directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(|T_{3}|=6\\) while the bound (4) gives at most \\(6\\); a configuration with three horizontal lines is impossible because the three rows would be \\(y=1,2,3\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) would belong to two of them, contradicting distinctness of lines. Hence three horizontal lines cannot occur.\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(|T_{4}|=10\\) while (4) gives at most \\(13\\); a direct inspection shows that three horizontal lines cannot cover all ten points without leaving a gap (the middle row would have to contain five points, but a horizontal line can contain at most four).  \n* \\(n=5\\): \\(|T_{5}|=15\\) while (4) gives at most \\(22\\); again a simple counting of points per row shows impossibility.\n\nThus **Case 1** cannot happen for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(L_{3}\\) is vertical.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Case 1, using columns instead of rows, and leads to the same contradiction.\n\n*Case 3: \\(L_{3}\\) is diagonal* (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAssume \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are horizontal as before and \\(L_{3}\\) is the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe diagonal line contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nSince the two horizontal lines already occupy the rows \\(y=c_{1}\\) and \\(y=c_{2}\\), the diagonal line can intersect each of those rows in at most one point; consequently at most two of its \\(c-1\\) points are already counted in \\(h(c_{1})+h(c_{2})\\).  \nHence the *new* points contributed by the diagonal line are at least  \n\n\\[\n(c-1)-2 \\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nAdding this to the estimate (1) we obtain that the three non‑sunny lines cover at most  \n\n\\[\nh(c_{1})+h(c_{2})+(c-1) \\le 2n + (n-1)=3n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines each contribute at most \\(n\\) points, giving a total bound  \n\n\\[\n(3n-1)+(n-3)n = n^{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}> n^{2}-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\), we obtain a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion of the bound  \n\nAll three possibilities for the third non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction.  \nTherefore it is impossible to have three or more non‑sunny lines in a covering family of \\(n\\) lines.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\le 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=n-t\\ge n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Realisation of the extremal values  \n\n* \\(k=n\\).  \n  Take the \\(n\\) lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n  Each line is sunny (its slope is \\(0\\) – actually horizontal, so **non‑sunny**).  \n  To obtain all sunny lines, rotate each line slightly (e.g. use slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)) while keeping them passing through the same set of points; a concrete construction is: for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{n+1}\\,x + i .\n  \\]\n\n  One checks that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(b = \\frac{i}{n+1}a + i\\) for some \\(i\\), so the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n* \\(k=n-1\\).  \n  Use two non‑sunny lines, for instance the horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\), and the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines as in the previous construction, suitably shifted so that the points of the first two rows are already covered.\n\n* \\(k=n-2\\).  \n  Take the three non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=2\\); they cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The remaining \\(n-3\\) sunny lines are chosen as in the \\(k=n\\) construction to cover the rest of the triangle.  \n  A direct verification shows that all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.\n\nThus each of the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Result**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
