y the sunny line \\(y = 2x-1\\).  \n     Every point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) lies on this new line, while \\((1,1)\\) is already covered by the line \\(y=x\\). Again the covering is preserved and we gain another sunny line.\n\n   After these two replacements we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\), \\(s=3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).\n\n   - **Inductive step.** Suppose we have a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) that covers \\(T_n\\). Choose any remaining non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) (it is either of the form \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=s\\)). Pick a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on \\(L\\) but is not the unique intersection of the already\u2011present sunny lines (such a point exists because each non\u2011sunny line contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) and at most one of them can be covered exclusively by the sunny lines). Replace \\(L\\) by the unique sunny line through \\(P\\) with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that does **not** coincide with any existing line. Because the new line passes through \\(P\\) and all other points formerly covered by \\(L\\) are still covered by the remaining lines (either they belong to another non\u2011sunny line or to a sunny line already present), the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\n   Repeating this replacement \\(r\\) times (where \\(0\\le r\\le n\\)) yields a configuration with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k = r\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines. Since \\(r\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\), we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion** \u2013 the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) is also sufficient; therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the boundary).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line?\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the case of a vertical line is covered by Lemma\u202f3 below).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) we have \\(b=ma+q\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Eliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+ma+q\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a\\le n+1-q .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq -1\\), the coefficient \\(1+m\\) is a non\u2011zero integer. Hence the integer values of \\(a\\) that satisfy the inequality form an interval of length at most \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-q}{|1+m|}\\).  \n\nNow consider the projection of the lattice points of \\(T_n\\) onto the direction orthogonal to \\(\\ell\\). The orthogonal projection of the whole triangle has length exactly \\(n\\). Along that direction the integer lattice points appear at distance \\(1\\) from each other, so any line of slope \\(m\\) can meet at most one lattice point for each two consecutive integer values of the projection coordinate. Consequently the number of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) cannot exceed half of the length of the projection, i.e. \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (non\u2011sunny lines)  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\) then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal* line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge1\\)): the condition \\(a+c\\le n+1\\) gives \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) possibilities.  \n*Vertical* line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \n*Line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge2\\); the points of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) and \\(d\\le n+1\\), giving exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) solutions. \u220e\n\n\n\n### 3.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor + tn .\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) and the trivial inequality \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\le \\frac{n+1}{2}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le s\\frac{n+1}{2}+ (n-s)n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=s\\le n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is sharp, as we shall see.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions achieving every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_{s}&: x+y=s \\qquad (s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe two axis\u2011parallel lines together with the \\(n-2\\) lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\) (the argument is the same as in the original answer). All lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines\n\nWe transform the above configuration step by step.\n\n*Step 1.* Replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) remains on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); every other point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\) and also lies on the line \\(y=x\\) after the replacement because \\(a=1\\) is the only integer for which \\(a\\neq b\\) on that line. Consequently all points formerly covered by \\(y=1\\) are still covered, and we have introduced one sunny line.\n\n*Step 2.* Replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_2:\\; y = 2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nNow the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) satisfy \\(b=2\\cdot1-1=1\\) only for \\(b=1\\); for \\(b\\ge2\\) they are covered by the line \\(x+y= b+1\\) (which is present among the lines \\(x+y=s\\)). The point \\((1,1)\\) is already on \\(S_1\\). Hence the covering is preserved and we have a second sunny line.\n\nAfter these two replacements we possess a configuration with exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines (\\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x-1\\)) and \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(s=3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).\n\n#### 4.3  General inductive step  \n\nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)) that covers \\(T_n\\). If \\(k=n\\) we are done. Otherwise there exists at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal C\\).  \n\nChoose a point \\(P\\in L\\cap T_n\\) that is **not** the unique intersection point of the already existing sunny lines (such a point exists because \\(L\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) while at most one of them can be the exclusive intersection of the sunny lines).  \n\nDefine a new line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; \\text{the unique line through }P\\text{ with slope }m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand with the additional requirement that \\(S\\) does not coincide with any line already present in \\(\\mathcal C\\) (this is always possible because the set of forbidden slopes is finite).  \n\nReplace \\(L\\) by \\(S\\).  \n\n*Why the covering is preserved.*  \n- The point \\(P\\) is now on \\(S\\).  \n- Every other point of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) either lies on another non\u2011sunny line (different from \\(L\\)) or on a sunny line already present; in both cases it remains covered.  \n- No new point is lost, and no new point is needed.\n\nThus we obtain a new configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. Repeating the step \\(n-k\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the inductive step works for any intermediate value of \\(k\\), we have constructed, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary condition (2) together with the explicit constructions shows that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
