 to that line yields a new covering \\(\\mathcal C_{r+1}\\) with \\(r+1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Repeating the step produces, for every integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\), a family \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) together with the constructive induction shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary condition\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nHence any admissible integer \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with no sunny line\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{v}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{h}&: y=1,\\\\\nL_{s}&: x+y=s \\qquad (s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (3) are distinct; their slopes are respectively \\(\\infty,0,-1\\), so none is sunny.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\{L_{v},L_{h}\\}\\cup\\{L_{s}\\mid s=4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{v}\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{h}\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\ge 4\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{a+b}\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Redundancy of the base configuration\n\nFor each diagonal line \\(L_{s}\\) (\\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\)) we have the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_{s}=(1,s-1)\\in L_{v}\\cap L_{s},\\qquad \nQ_{s}=(s-1,1)\\in L_{h}\\cap L_{s}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence **every** non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) contains a point that also belongs to another line of the family.  \nWe shall use this property in the replacement step.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Replacement Lemma\n\n> **Lemma 4.1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\). Then there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) such that  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{S\\}\n> \\]\n> also covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nBy the redundancy property (which holds for \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) and is preserved under the replacement operation, see below), there exists a point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in L\\cap T_{n}\n\\]\n\nthat lies on another line \\(L'\\in\\mathcal C\\), \\(L'\\neq L\\).\n\nConsider the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma=\\{\\text{slope of }M\\mid M\\in\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{L\\}\\}\\cup\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\mu\\notin\\Sigma\\); such a \\(\\mu\\) exists because \\(\\Sigma\\) is finite.  \nLet \\(S\\) be the unique line through \\(P\\) with slope \\(\\mu\\). Then \\(S\\) is sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nNow examine any point \\(Q\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(Q\\notin L\\) then the line(s) covering \\(Q\\) are unchanged in \\(\\mathcal C'\\).\n* If \\(Q\\in L\\) and \\(Q=P\\) then \\(Q\\) lies on the new line \\(S\\).\n* If \\(Q\\in L\\) and \\(Q\\neq P\\) then, by the choice of \\(P\\), \\(Q\\) is also on the other line \\(L'\\) that remains in \\(\\mathcal C'\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) remains covered after the replacement, and the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Inductive construction of all values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exhibited \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) with \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nAssume that for some \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r<n\\) we have a covering \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) of \\(T_{n}\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct lines and containing exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(r<n\\), \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) contains at least one non\u2011sunny line; applying Lemma\u202f4.1 to that line yields a new covering \\(\\mathcal C_{r+1}\\) with \\(r+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal C_{r}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and having exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (2) and the constructive induction together give the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]"

