figuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n**Statement.** For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and none of them is sunny.\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the following set of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,y=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\((n-2)+(n-2)+1 = 2n-3\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\); we shall select any \\(n\\) of them that contain the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nTake\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y_{\\,n-2},\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x_{\\,n-2},\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\n\nNow we verify (1).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\n*If \\(a+b\\le n\\)*, then either \\(a\\le n-2\\) or \\(b\\le n-2\\) (or both).  \n\n– If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\n– If \\(b\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) which also belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, (1) holds and the configuration has \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of configurations with any \\(k\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\))\n\n**Statement.** Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBegin with the configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) from Lemma 1, which contains \\(n\\) lines, none sunny.  \nWe shall replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y_{\