hen \\(a'\\ge n-k\\) and\n  \\((a',b)\\in D\\). Since \\((a',b)\\neq Q_i\\) for all \\(i\\) (otherwise we would\n  already be in Case 1), we have \\(a'\\le n-k-1\\) and consequently\n  \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (because \\(a\\le a'\\) and both\n  share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate). In any sub‑case the point is covered.\n\nThus every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists. Therefore the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
