resent.  To avoid this\n  difficulty we modify the base family as follows: replace the missing\n  vertical \\(x=1\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (already present) and note\n  that every point with \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(1+b\\le n+1\\), i.e.\n  \\(b\\le n\\).  Such a point either lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) (when\n  \\(b=n\\)) or on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  For \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\) the line\n  \\(y=b\\) coincides with the vertical line \\(v_b\\) after a 90° rotation; in\n  our present configuration we do not have those horizontals, but we can\n  observe that the point \\((1,b)\\) also belongs to the line \\(v_{b}\\) after a\n  reflection across the line \\(x=y\\).  To keep the argument elementary we\n  simply add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) to the family and delete the horizontal\n  line \\(h\\); the resulting family still has \\(n\\) lines and the covering\n  argument becomes straightforward.  Consequently we adopt the following\n  *final* base family.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B'=\\{d,\\;x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(|\\mathcal B'|=2+(n-3)=n\\).  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\):\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) only\n  when \\(a=n\\); otherwise it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is\n  absent from \\(\\mathcal B'\\).  To remedy this we keep the horizontal line\n  \\(y=1\\) instead of one of the other horizontals; the precise choice is\n  irrelevant because all horizontals are interchangeable.  Hence we finally\n  take  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal B''=\\{d,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\}\\cup\\{y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}},\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhich again contains exactly \\(n\\) lines.  The covering verification is now\ntrivial:\n\n* Points with \\(a=1\\) are on \\(x=1\\);\n* Points with \\(b=1\\) are on \\(y=1\\);\n* Points with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(b\\le n-2\\) (otherwise\n  \\(a+b>n+1\\) or the point lies on the diagonal \\(d\\)).  Hence such a point\n  lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that belongs to \\(\\mathcal B''\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal B''\\) indeed covers all of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Introducing sunny lines  \n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal B''\\) contains exactly three non‑sunny lines\n(\\(d,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\)) and \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines, all non‑sunny.  \nTo obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\nwe replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines by sunny lines, keeping the two\nmandatory non‑sunny lines \\(d\\) and \\(x=1\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{\\,y=t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\,\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal B''\\) (there are \\(n-3\\) of them).\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subset H\\) with \\(|S|=k\\) (possible because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).\nFor each \\(y=t\\in S\\) pick a distinct slope \\(\\alpha_t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_t\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=\\alpha_t x + (t-\\alpha_t\\cdot 1),\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the point \\((1,t)\\).  The point \\((1,t)\\) is already\ncovered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\); therefore after we delete the horizontal\nline \\(y=t\\) and insert \\(\\ell_t\\) the covering of \\((1,t)\\) is preserved.\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) that were on \\(y=t\\) have abscissa \\(a\\ge 2\\); such\npoints also lie on the diagonal \\(d\\) when \\(a+t=n+1\\) or on another horizontal\nline \\(y=t'\\) with \\(t'\\neq t\\).  Consequently none of them loses a covering\nwhen \\(y=t\\) is removed.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal B''\\setminus S\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_t\\mid y=t\\in S\\}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \n\\(\\mathcal B''\\) has \\(n\\) lines; we delete \\(|S|=k\\) lines and add the same\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines, so \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) are sunny; all other lines are\nparallel to one of the forbidden directions.  Hence \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nWe have just argued that every point of \\(T_n\\) remains on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  Therefore \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) satisfies the required condition.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\),\nall these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 2.1 gives the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit families \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) constructed above show that every\n\\(k\\) in this range can indeed be realized.  Consequently the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]"
