 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no horizontal line, then any line meeting two\ndifferent points of \\(R_1\\) would have to be horizontal, a contradiction.\nThus each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain at most one point of \\(R_1\\),\nand covering all \\(n\\) points of \\(R_1\\) would again require at least \\(n\\)\nlines, which is impossible.  Therefore a horizontal line is mandatory\n(the same argument works with the first column and yields a vertical line if\nwe prefer a vertical one).\n\n∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 we obtain the **necessary inequality**\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIndeed the two lines guaranteed by the lemma are not sunny, so at most the\nremaining \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A base family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nDefine the following three lines:\n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1,\\qquad\nh:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nv_t:\\;x=t\\;(2\\le t\\le n-2).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{d,h\\}\\cup\\{v_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal B\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B|=2+(n-3)=n .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll lines of \\(\\mathcal B\\) are distinct and non‑sunny (the first two are\nparallel to the forbidden directions, the verticals are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis).\n\n**Claim.**  \\(\\mathcal B\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in h\\).\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\(a\\le n-2\\) is impossible because \\(a=1\\); nevertheless\n  \\((1,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(d\\) when \\(b=n\\) and otherwise on the\n  horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which coincides with \\(h\\) only for \\(b=1\\).  In\n  the latter case \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n\\); the\n  point \\((1,b)\\) is on the diagonal \\(d\\) exactly when \\(b=n\\); otherwise\n  it is on the vertical line \\(v_1\\) which is **not** present