very point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nU\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad |U|=n-2-k .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k:=\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_t\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus U\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) still has \\(n\\) distinct lines; exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_t\\) with \\(t\\notin U\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) has\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe covering argument of Section 2 remains valid because the points that were\ncovered exclusively by a removed line \\(\\sigma_t\\) (with \\(t\\in U\\)) are of the\nform \\((t,t-1)\\) or \\((t-1,t)\\); each such point also lies on the horizontal\nline \\(\\ell_h\\) (when \\(t=2\\)) or on the vertical line \\(\\ell_v\\) (when\n\\(t=n-1\\)).  For all other removed lines the points they would have covered\nare already covered by another line \\(\\sigma_{t'}\\) with \\(t'\\neq t\\).  Hence\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered.\n\nTherefore for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the problem conditions and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must contain at least two\nnon‑sunny lines, giving the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit families \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) constructed above demonstrate that every\ninteger \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Consequently the set\nof all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour 