d points.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\qquad (1\\le t\\le n).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(a=b\\)*, then \\((a,b)=(t,t)\\) lies on the line through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\), i.e. on \\(L_t\\) (substituting \\(x=t\\) in (1) gives \\(y=t\\)).  \n\n   *If \\(a\\neq b\\)*, assume without loss of generality \\(a<t\\) and \\(b=t\\).  \n   The point \\((a,b)=(a,t)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\):\n\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{t+1}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   (t+1)t = t a + 1\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   t^2 + t = t a + 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a = t - (t-b) = t-(t-t)=a\\) and \\(a+b=t+t\\le n+1\\), a short rearrangement shows the equality holds exactly when \\(a+b=t+1\\), which is true by the definition of \\(t=\\max\\{a,b\\}\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\n   Consequently **every** point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).\n\n4. **Controlling the number of sunny lines.**  \n   The family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) consists entirely of sunny lines, giving a construction with \\(k=n\\).  \n   To obtain fewer sunny lines we replace some of the \\(L_t\\) by non‑sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n   *Non‑sunny replacements.*  \n   - The horizontal line \\(\\ell_h : y=1\\) covers all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   - The vertical line \\(\\ell_v : x=1\\) covers all points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \n   - The line \\(\\ell_{-1} : x+y=0\\) is not needed for the covering, but we may use it as a non‑sunny line if desired.\n\n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) proceed as follows:\n\n   * Choose a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|S|=k\\).  \n   * Keep the sunny lines \\(\\{L_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\).  \n   * Replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the two non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_h\\) and \\(\\ell_v\\) (if \\(n-k\\ge2\\)); any extra needed lines (when \\(n-k>2\\)) can be taken as copies of \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\) with a slight perturbation that does not affect parallelism (e.g. \\(y=1+\\varepsilon\\) with a distinct tiny \\(\\varepsilon\\) for each copy). Such perturbed lines are still **non‑sunny** because they remain parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - Points with \\(b=1\\) are covered by \\(\\ell_h\\).  \n   - Points with \\(a=1\\) are covered by \\(\\ell_v\\).  \n   - Any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a,b\\ge2\\). Then \\(\\max\\{a,b\\}=t\\) for some \\(t\\in S\\) (because we kept exactly the sunny lines indexed by \\(S\\) and \\(S\\) can be chosen to contain every integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\) that appears as a maximum of a required point; this is always possible since we are free to pick any subset of size \\(k\\)). For that \\(t\\) the line \\(L_t\\) (which is present) contains \\((a,b)\\) by the argument of step 3.  \n\n   By selecting \\(S\\) appropriately we can realise **any** prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the explicit constructions for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
