ailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A construction with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently it is **not** sunny.  \nThe lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are distinct because they have different constant terms.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No configuration can contain a sunny line\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line.  \nLet \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a sunny line. By definition, \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal, slope \\(0\\));\n* the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical, undefined slope);\n* the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nHence the slope of \\(\\ell\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical.\n\nConsider the partition of \\(T_n\\) according to the value of the sum \\(a+b\\):\n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} S_s,\\qquad \nS_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach set \\(S_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points.  \nObserve that any line **n