\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of  \n\\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (diagonal).  \nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line is parallel to one of these three directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) lay on a horizontal line they would have the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, contradicting the fact that their sums \\(x+y\\) are distinct (otherwise the line would be diagonal). The same argument works for vertical and diagonal lines, using the distinctness of the \\(x\\)-coordinates, \\(y\\)-coordinates and sums respectively. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThese three points determine a unique line; the direction vector of that line must be a linear combination of the differences of the points. Because the three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\), the direction vector would be forced to be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, contradicting the definition of sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a configuration satisfying the problem consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points. Hence\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)=k+n .\n\\]\n\nUsing (2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le k+n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n