            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution of the problem.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Upper bound.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_1:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad d_2:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad d_3:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line is sunny, then among the points of \\(T_n\\) it can contain at most two\npoints whose sums \\(a+b\\) are equal, because otherwise the line would be parallel\nto \\(d_3\\).  Consequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of the same\ndiagonal \\(x+y=s\\).  Since there are \\(n\\) different diagonals\n\\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), the total number of points that can be covered by the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nAll remaining points, at least \\(|T_n|-2k\\) of them, must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Each non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three\nforbidden directions; in particular a line parallel to \\(d_3\\) coincides with a\nwhole diagonal and therefore covers **all** points of that diagonal.  There are\nexactly \\(n\\) diagonals, so the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most\n\\(n-k\\) whole diagonals, i.e. at most \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) points.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;2k+(n-k)(n-1).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward simplification yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n*Construction.*  \nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nChoose the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-2i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nwhich lie on distinct diagonals \\(x+y=n+1-i\\).\nFor each \\(i\\) draw the sunny line \\(L_i\\) through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-2i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,n-2i).\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(L_i\\) is \\((1,-1)\\) shifted, so \\(L_i\\) is not parallel\nto any of the three forbidden directions; hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct and together cover exactly the\n\\(2k\\) points \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\mid1\\le i\\le k\\}\\).\n\nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad\\text{for }s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n+1-i\\mid1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-k\\) such diagonals.  For each of them we take the line\n\\(D_s:\\;x+y=s\\); this line is parallel to the forbidden direction \\(d_3\\) and\nhence is non‑sunny.  The lines \\(D_s\\) are distinct and together with the sunny\nlines give a total of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) (and\nhence to a sunny line) or to one of the remaining diagonals (and hence to a\nnon‑sunny line).  Thus all required points are covered, and exactly \\(k\\) of the\nlines are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) in the range\n\\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and the upper‑bound argument shows\nthat no larger \\(k\\) is possible, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\nprecisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
