\\]\n\nbe the set of points not already covered by the two sunny lines.  For each\n\\(Q\\in U\\) choose **one** line from \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) that\ncontains \\(Q\\); this is always possible by the definition of the three families.\nThus we obtain a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\subset\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal\nD\\) that covers \\(U\\).\n\nWe now bound \\(|\\mathcal L|\\).  Every line of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal\nD\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the intersection of the line with\n\\(\\Delta_n\\) is a segment of length at most \\(n\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\le\\frac{|U|}{1}\\le\\frac{|T_n|}{1}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound is obtained by observing that each line of the three families\ncovers **at least** one new point of \\(U\\); therefore the greedy choice described\nabove yields a covering with at most \\(|U|\\) lines.  Since the two sunny lines\nalready contain at most \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (each contains at most \\(n\\)\npoints and they intersect at \\(P\\)), we have\n\n\\[\n|U| = |T_n| - |S_1\\cup S_2|\n      \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - (2n-1)\n      = \\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}2 \\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nThus we can select at most \\(n-2\\) lines from the three families that together\ncover all points of \\(U\\).  Adding the two sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\) we obtain a\nfamily of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and the\nfamily contains precisely two sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Configurations with one or zero sunny lines)\n\n*From the construction of Lemma 2 we can obtain configurations with exactly\n\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) produced in Lemma 2, which consists of the\ntwo sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2\\) together with a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of at most\n\\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines covering the remaining points.\n\n*One sunny line.*  Delete, say, \\(S_1\\).  The points that were on \\(S_1\\) are\nstill covered because each such point also lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\),\n\\(\\mathcal V\\) or \\(\\mathcal D\\) (the three families intersect \\(S_1\\) in a\nlattice point, and that intersecting line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\)).  The\nresulting family has at most \\(n-1\\) lines; add any line from the three families\nthat is not already present.  The final family has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines\nand contains precisely the sunny line \\(S_2\\).\n\n*No sunny line.*  Delete both \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_2\\).  The remaining set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) because, as argued above,\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of the three families, and the greedy\nselection in Lemma 2 guarantees a covering with at most \\(n-2\\) such lines.\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L|<n\\), add arbitrary new lines from the three families (still\nnon‑sunny) until the total number of lines becomes \\(n\\).  The resulting\nconfiguration contains no sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le2\\).  \nLemmas 2 and 3 exhibit explicit configurations for \\(k=2,1,0\\).  Therefore the\ncomplete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
