e slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\)) or on the diagonal line \\(D\\) (the unique point with \\(x=n\\)).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\\qquad\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(1+(n-1-(k+1)+1)=n-k-1\\) such lines; all are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Sunny lines*  \n   For each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y-1=j\\,(x-j) .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(j\\neq0,-1\\); thus every \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n   *Counting*  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(D)+\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{vertical }V_i}+k\\;(S_j)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   - **Case\u202fA.** \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \n     Then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\le n-1\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   - **Case\u202fB.** \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  \n     Write \\(b=1\\) or \\(b>1\\).\n\n     *Subcase\u202fB1.* \\(b=1\\).  \n     Substituting \\((x,y)=(a,1)\\) into (2) with \\(j=a\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     1-1=a\\,(a-a)=0,\n     \\]\n\n     so \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\n     *Subcase\u202fB2.* \\(b>1\\).  \n     Because \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n     \u2013 If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n     \u2013 Otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Set \\(j=b-1\\) (note that \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) because\n       \\(b\\le n-a\\le n-(k+1)\\le k+1\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\)).  Substituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into (2) with this \\(j\\) yields  \n\n     \\[\n     b-1=(b-1)\