llowed because the lines are distinct. Consequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on its column’s vertical line, and the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus the family consisting of  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\), and  \n* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\),\n\ncovers all points of \\(S\\).  Since the vertical lines are not sunny, the number of sunny lines in the family is precisely \\(k\\).  Removing any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines (for instance those whose columns are used for the sunny lines) leaves exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines in total, still covering \\(S\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0<k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all sunny, that covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\):\n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,1)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset S .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never horizontal, therefore each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(H\\).  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(H\\) must lie on \\(n\\) distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\); in other words the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are exactly the \\(n\\) distinct lines each passing through a different point of \\(H\\).\n\nNow look at the \\(n\\) points on the vertical line \\(x=1\\):\n\n\\[\nV=\\{(1,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset S .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, so none of the lines already used to cover \\(H\\) can contain any point of \\(V\\).  \nThus covering the points of \\(V\\) would require another set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, contradicting the assumption that only \\(n\\) lines are available.\n\nHence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) and proved that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
