al L\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n-s .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\); hence at most \\(n-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(n-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and a slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,n+1-c\\}\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSumming these maximal contributions we obtain an upper bound for \\(|T_n|\\) in terms of \\(h,v,d\\).  \nA straightforward calculation (see Lemma 1 below) shows that the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(h\\) horizontals, \\(v\\) verticals and \\(d\\) slope‑\\(-1\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le (h+v+d)(n-2)-\\frac{(h+v+d)(h+v+d-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\), the inequality forces  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(s=n-(h+v+d)\\le n-2\\).  \n\nHence **no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Choose the following lines*  \n\n1. **Two vertical lines**  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n   (Both are non‑sunny.)\n\n2. **\\(n-2-k\\) horizontal lines**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) and \\(c\\neq n-k\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\;y=c .\n   \\]\n   There are exactly \\(n-2-k\\) such values of \\(c\\); all these lines are non‑sunny.\n\n3. **\\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) define  \n   \\[\n   m_t:\\;x+y=t .\n   \\]\n   Each \\(m_t\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore sunny.\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-2-k)+k=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(m_t\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on one of the lines  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Three mutually exclusive cases cover all possibilities.\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=1\\) or \\(a=n\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\), respectively.\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\) equals one of the chosen horizontal constants.*  \nThe set of horizontal constants is \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{n-k\\}\\).  \nIf \\(b\\) belongs to this set, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b}\\).\n\n*Case 3: Neither of the above.*  \nThus \\(a\\neq 1,n\\) and \\(b\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{n-k\\}\\).  \nSince \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), the only remaining possibility is \\(b=n-k\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \nHence \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(k+1\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=a+b\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\), and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(m_{a+b}\\).\n\nIn every case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  Hence the covering condition holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma used in the upper‑bound argument  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(h\\) horizontals, \\(v\\) verticals and \\(d\\) slope \\(-1\\) lines be chosen arbitrarily.  \nThe total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by these lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n(h+v+d)(n-2)-\\frac{(h+v+d)(h+v+d-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nOrder the lines arbitrarily and add them one by one.  \nWhen the first line is added it can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nEach subsequent line can intersect the already placed lines in at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore it can contribute at most \\((n-2)-(\\text{number of previously placed lines})\\) new points.  \nSumming the geometric series yields the claimed bound. ∎\n\nApplying Lemma 1 with \\(h+v+d=n-s\\) and using \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le (n-s)(n-2)-\\frac{(n-s)(n-s-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(s\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
