ause \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na+b \\ge n-k+2.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\), the lower bound \\(n-k+2\\) is **greater than \\(k+1\\)** for all but the smallest values of \\(n\\). For example, with \\(n=6,\\;k=2\\) we have \\(b=4\\) and possible \\(a\\) values \\(1,2,3\\); the sums are \\(5,6,7\\), none of which lie in \\(\\{2,3\\}\\). Thus the point \\((a,b)=(2,4)\\in T_6\\) is **not** covered by any line of \\(\\mathcal L_2\\).  \n\nHence the claim that every point with \\(b=n-k\\) lies on a sunny line \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) is false. The construction does **not** satisfy the covering condition for general \\(n,k\\). → **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Conclusion drawn from the (flawed) upper bound and construction  \n\nBecause both the upper bound \\(s\\le n-2\\) and the existence of configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) are unsupported, the final statement that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) is **unproved** and, in fact, false.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains multiple critical mistakes:\n\n1. Incorrect enumeration of the set \\(T_n\\).  \n2. Wrong maximal number of lattice points a non‑sunny line can contain.  \n3. Faulty combinatorial bound leading to an unjustified inequality \\(s\\le n-2\\).  \n4. A construction that fails to cover all required points for many choices of \\(k\\).\n\nThese errors invalidate the entire argument. The problem remains unsolved by the presented solution."
