two lines, and both lines are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth lines pass through the right‑most point \\((n+1-i,i)\\) of \\(H_i\\).  \nFor an integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\),\n\n\\[\n(a,i)\\in S_{i,1}\\iff i-i=\\alpha\\bigl(a-(n+2-i)\\bigr)\n\\iff a\\equiv n+2-i\\pmod{\\alpha},\n\\]\n\nand similarly for \\(S_{i,2}\\) with modulus \\(\\beta\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are consecutive, every integer in the interval \\([1,n+1-i]\\) satisfies at least one of the two congruences; consequently each point of \\(H_i\\) lies on \\(S_{i,1}\\) or on \\(S_{i,2}\\).  \nBoth slopes \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line by one sunny line and one vertical line).  \nWith the same notation, let  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-i=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(n+2-i)\\bigr),\\qquad V_i:\\; x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\alpha\\ge2\\). Then every point of \\(H_i\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) or on \\(V_i\\); exactly one of the two lines is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(S_i\\) passes through \\((n+1-i,i)\\). For any \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n+1-i\\),\n\n\\[\n(a,i)\\in S_i\\iff a\\equiv n+2-i\\pmod{\\alpha},\n\\]\n\nwhich holds because the interval length is at most \\(\\alpha\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((1,i)\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(V_i\\).  \n\\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (sunny) while \\(V_i\\) is vertical (non‑sunny). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 4 (Existence for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBegin with the base configuration of Lemma 1 (all lines non‑sunny, \\(k=0\\)).  \n\n*Adding two sunny lines.*  \nSelect a horizontal line \\(y=i\\) that has not yet been replaced and apply Lemma 2: delete \\(y=i\\) and insert the two sunny lines \\(S_{i,1},S_{i,2}\\). The total number of lines remains \\(n\\) and the sunny count increases by two.\n\n*Adding one sunny line.*  \nIf an odd increase is required, first apply Lemma 3 to a remaining horizontal line \\(y=j\\): replace it by the sunny line \\(S_j\\) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\). This raises the sunny count by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nBy performing a suitable combination of the “add‑two’’ and “add‑one’’ operations we can achieve any prescribed increase \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). Each replacement covers exactly the same set of points of \\(T_n\\) as the line it replaces, so the covering condition is preserved throughout. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 5 (Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)).  \nNo configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines exists. Since a sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), at most three lines in the configuration can be non‑sunny.\n\n1. **Diagonal points.**  \nThe set \\(D=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points. A sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D\\); otherwise it would be parallel to the diagonal. Hence at least \\(n-3\\) sunny lines are needed to cover the diagonal points that are not covered by the at‑most‑three non‑sunny lines.\n\n2. **Horizontal points.**  \nThe set \\(H=\\{(a,1)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) also contains \\(n\\) points. Again a sunny line can contain at most one of them. The only non‑sunny line that can help is a vertical line, which can cover at most one point of \\(H\\). Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) additional sunny lines