            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). They cover all required lattice points and none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **\\(0<k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   * Choose \\(k\\) distinct columns \\(a_{1},\\dots ,a_{k}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(k\\) distinct rows \\(b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with the property that for every \\(i\\) the pair \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\neq(1,1)\\) and \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\neq n+1\\).  \n   * For each \\(i\\) draw the line  \n     \\[\n     L_{i}:\\;y-b_{i}=m_{i}\\,(x-a_{i}),\\qquad \n     m_{i}=\\frac{b_{i}-1}{1-a_{i}} .\n     \\]\n     The line \\(L_{i}\\) passes through the two points \\((a_{i},1)\\) and \\((1,b_{i})\\); consequently it contains **all** points of column \\(a_{i}\\) that belong to the set  \n     \\[\n     S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     \\]\n     and also all points of row \\(b_{i}\\) that belong to \\(S\\).  \n     Because \\(a_{i}\\neq1\\) and \\(b_{i}\\neq1\\), the slope \\(m_{i}\\) is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_{i}\\) is **sunny**.  \n   * Let  \n     \\[\n     C=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{a_{1},\\dots ,a_{k}\\}.\n     \\]\n     For every \\(c\\in C\\) draw the vertical line \\(V_{c}:x=c\\). These \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny.  \n\n   The family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{V_{c}\\mid c\\in C\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   *Coverage.*  \n   – If a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies in a column \\(a=a_{i}\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) (by construction of \\(L_{i}\\)).  \n   – If \\(a\\notin\\{a_{1},\\dots ,a_{k}\\}\\) then \\(a\\in C\\) and the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny. Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0<k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n3. **\\(k=n\\) is impossible.**  \n   Suppose that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) cover all points of \\(S\\).  \n\n   *Horizontal points.*  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   H=\\{(a,1)\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset S\n   \\]\n   lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). A sunny line is never horizontal, therefore each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(H\\). Consequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(H\\) must lie on \\(n\\) distinct lines among \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\).  \n\n   *Vertical points.*  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   V=\\{(1,b)\\mid b=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset S\n   \\]\n   lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\). A sunny line is never vertical, so none of the lines already used to cover the points of \\(H\\) can contain any point of \\(V\\) **except possibly the common point \\((1,1)\\)**, which belongs to both \\(H\\) and \\(V\\). Because the lines covering \\(H\\) are all distinct and each already contains a different point of \\(H\\), at most one of them can pass through \\((1,1)\\). Hence at most one point of \\(V\\) is already covered.  \n\n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(V\\) each require a distinct sunny line (a sunny line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point). Therefore we need at least \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) lines **in addition to** the \\(n\\) lines already used for \\(H\\), i.e. at least \\(2n-1\\) distinct lines, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are available.  \n\n   Hence a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist; the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\nCombining the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) with the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
