rwise we would have at most \\((n-2)+(n-2)+1=2n-3\\) covered points, which is insufficient). Hence at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny.  \n\n   Moreover, every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Such a point lies on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=a+b,\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny. Consequently **all** lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are forced to be present in any admissible family; there are exactly \\(n-2\\) of them.  \n\n   Adding the two forced non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) (which cover the whole first row and first column) gives a total of \\(n\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny. Hence no sunny line can appear, i.e. \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n4. **Construction showing that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.**  \n\n   Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * the vertical line \\(\\ell_{V}:x=1\\);\n   * the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{H}:y=1\\);\n   * for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n+1\\) the diagonal line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_{c}:x+y=c .\n     \\]\n\n   The \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct and each of them is non\u2011sunny (they are respectively parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   **Coverage verification.**  \n   - Any point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{V}\\) if \\(a=1\\) and on \\(\\ell_{H}\\) otherwise.  \n   - Any point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{H}\\) if \\(b=1\\) and on \\(\\ell_{V}\\) otherwise.  \n   - For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\(c:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=c\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{c}\\).\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and the family contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. 