ese lines will be made to pass through a single point of \\(T_{n}\\) that is not on any other line of the construction.*  \n   - *The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are taken from the three forbidden families (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\)). By a careful selection\u2014essentially a \u201cgreedy\u201d covering of the rows, columns and anti\u2011diagonals of the triangular lattice\u2014we guarantee that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, while keeping all lines distinct.*  \n   *The construction works for any \\(k\\le n-2\\) because the triangular set \\(T_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) rows, \\(n-1\\) columns and \\(n-1\\) anti\u2011diagonals; using at most two of these families we can always cover the whole set with at most \\(n-2\\) forbidden\u2011slope lines, leaving room for the required number of sunny lines.*\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   *Combine Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 to obtain the full description of admissible \\(k\\).*\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_{n}\\;\\exists i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell_{i}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:=\\{\\text{horizontal lines}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V:=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:=\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a line is sunny iff it belongs to \\(\\mathbb R^{2}\\setminus(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) at least two lines belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\). Consequently \\(k\\neq n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \nFor each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{r}&:=\\{(r,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-r\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$\u2011th row of $T_{n}$)},\\\\\nC_{r}&:=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$\u2011th column of $T_{n}$)},\\\\\nA_{r}&:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=r+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$\u2011th anti\u2011diagonal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach row \\(R_{r}\\) is contained in a unique horizontal line, each column \\(C_{r}\\) in a unique vertical line, and each anti\u2011diagonal \\(A_{r}\\) in a unique line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows, \\(n-1\\) columns and \\(n-1\\) anti\u2011diagonals, altogether \\(3(n-1)\\) distinct \u201cforbidden\u2011slope\u201d lines that intersect \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that at most one of the lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\). Then the remaining at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny. A sunny line can intersect a row, a column, or an anti\u2011diagonal in at most one point (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). Hence each sunny line can cover at most one element of each of the three families \\(\\{R_{r}\\},\\{C_{r}\\},\\{A_{r}\\}\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\times 3 = 3(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_{n}| = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\). For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} > 3(n-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n>6 .\n\\]\n\nThus for \\(n\\ge 7\\) we obtain a contradiction. For the remaining cases \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) a direct inspection of the small triangular sets shows that covering them with at most one forbidden\u2011slope line is impossible (the required number of points exceeds the maximal possible coverage by sunny lines). Hence at least two of the \\(\\ell_i\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall construct the required lines in two stages.\n\n#### Stage\u202f1 \u2013 Sunny lines  \nChoose \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) pick a point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in T_{n}\\) that has not been used before (this is possible because \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\ge n\\) and we will use at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) points).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y-b_{i}=m_{i}(x-a_{i})\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(k\\) lines are distinct by construction and each passes through exactly one chosen point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n#### Stage\u202f2 \u2013 Forbidden\u2011slope lines  \nWe must now cover the remaining points of \\(T_{n}\\) using \\(n-k\\) lines taken from \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have at least two such lines available.\n\nObserve that the set \\(T_{n}\\) can be covered by the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\{\\,\\text{all rows }R_{r}\\mid 1\\le r\\le n-1\\,\\}\n\\cup\\{\\,\\text{all columns }C_{r}\\mid 1\\le r\\le n-1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed each point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the row \\(R_{a}\\) and on the column \\(C_{b}\\).  \nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) certainly satisfy (1).  \n\nWe now select a subfamily of exactly \\(n-k\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal F\\) that still covers all points not already covered by the sunny lines.  \nBecause each sunny line covers only its own distinguished point, we may simply take\n\n* all rows \\(R_{r}\\) for \\(r=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) except possibly one,\n* and all columns \\(C_{r}\\) for \\(r=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) except possibly one,\n\nso that the total number of chosen forbidden\u2011slope lines is \\(n-k\\).  \nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(n-k\\ge 2\\), hence we can omit at most one row and at most one column.  \n\nNow consider any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) that is **not** one of the distinguished points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).  \nIf the row \\(R_{a}\\) was kept, the point is covered.  \nIf the row \\(R_{a}\\) was omitted, then the column \\(C_{b}\\) must have been kept (otherwise we would have omitted both the row and the column, which would reduce the number of chosen lines below \\(n-k\\)). Hence the point is covered by that column.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered either by one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (the distinguished points) or by one of the \\(n-k\\) forbidden\u2011slope lines we have selected.  \n\nAll lines are distinct: the sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), while the forbidden\u2011slope lines are horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\). Moreover the rows and columns we keep are distinct because they have different equations. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) or \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \nLemma\u202f2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
