            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{r}&:=\\{(r,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-r\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$‑th row)},\\\\\nC_{r}&:=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$‑th column)},\\\\\nA_{r}&:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=r+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}\\quad\\text{(the $r$‑th anti‑diagonal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(R_{r}\\) lies on a unique horizontal line, each \\(C_{r}\\) on a unique vertical line, and each \\(A_{r}\\) on a unique line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nThus there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows, \\(n-1\\) columns and \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals that intersect \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_{n}\\;\\exists\\; \\text{a line of the collection containing }(a,b),\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nat least two of the lines belong to the set of forbidden‑slope lines \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines obeys  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that at most one line of the collection has a forbidden slope.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Observation.*  \nA sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of the same row, because that would make the line horizontal; similarly it cannot contain two points of the same column, nor two points of the same anti‑diagonal. Therefore a sunny line meets each row, each column and each anti‑diagonal in **at most one** point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nConsider the rows. There are \\(n-1\\) rows \\(R_{1},\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\).  \nIf the unique forbidden‑slope line (if it exists) is horizontal, it can cover an entire row; otherwise no row is covered completely.  \nAll remaining points of a row must be supplied by sunny lines, each providing at most one point of that row.  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is at most the number of rows, i.e. \\(n-1\\) points per row summed over all rows, which equals \\(|T_{n}|\\) **only if** every row is completely covered by the (possible) horizontal line.  \nIf the single forbidden line is not horizontal, then every row must be covered point‑wise by sunny lines, giving at most one point per row per sunny line. With \\(n-1\\) sunny lines the maximal number of points that can be covered is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\times (n-1)= (n-1)^{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1)  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} > (n-1)^{2}\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n}{2}>n-1\\) is equivalent to \\(n>2\\).  \nThus a contradiction arises unless the unique forbidden line is horizontal and it covers an entire row.\n\nNow suppose the unique forbidden line is horizontal and covers, say, row \\(R_{r_{0}}\\).  \nAll other rows \\(R_{r}\\;(r\\neq r_{0})\\) must be covered by sunny lines, each providing at most one point per row. Consequently the number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n|R_{r_{0}}|+\\sum_{r\\neq r_{0}} (n-1)=|R_{r_{0}}|+(n-2)(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|R_{r_{0}}|=n+1-r_{0}\\le n\\), we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\n\\text{covered points}\\le n+(n-2)(n-1)=n^{2}-3n+2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>n^{2}-3n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is easily verified (the inequality reduces to \\(n^{2}-5n+4>0\\), true for \\(n\\ge 5\\); the cases \\(n=3,4\\) can be checked directly).  \nHence even with a horizontal line we cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) using only one forbidden‑slope line.\n\nThe same argument works if the unique forbidden line is vertical or of slope \\(-1\\) (by symmetry).  \n\nTherefore at least two lines of the collection must belong to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (2) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 – Choose \\(k\\) reserved points.**  \nBecause \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\ge n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), we can select \\(k\\) distinct points  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(a_{i},b_{i})\\in T_{n}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nwith the additional property that no two of them lie in the same row, column or anti‑diagonal.  \nThis is possible: start with any point, then repeatedly choose a point that avoids the rows, columns and anti‑diagonals already used; at each step at most three rows, three columns and three anti‑diagonals are forbidden, leaving plenty of points because \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 – Sunny lines.**  \nPick \\(k\\) distinct admissible slopes \\(m_{1},\\dots ,m_{k}\\) (any real numbers different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y-b_{i}=m_{i}(x-a_{i})\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{i}\\) passes through exactly the reserved point \\(P_{i}\\) and through no other reserved point (different slopes guarantee distinctness).  \nAll \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are sunny by construction.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3 – Forbidden‑slope lines covering the remaining points.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{R_{r}\\mid 1\\le r\\le n-1\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal C:=\\{C_{r}\\mid 1\\le r\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the choice in Step 1, the rows \\(\\{R_{a_{i}}\\}\\) and columns \\(\\{C_{b_{i}}\\}\\) are all distinct, and none of the anti‑diagonals \\(A_{a_{i}+b_{i}-1}\\) coincides with another.  \n\nWe now keep **all** rows except those that contain a reserved point, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R^{*}:=\\mathcal R\\setminus\\{R_{a_{1}},\\dots ,R_{a_{k}}\\},\n\\]\n\nand similarly keep all columns except those that contain a reserved point,\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C^{*}:=\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{C_{b_{1}},\\dots ,C_{b_{k}}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe total number of kept forbidden‑slope lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R^{*}|+|\\mathcal C^{*}|=(n-1-k)+(n-1-k)=2(n-1-k).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\n2(n-1-k)\\ge n-k,\n\\]\n\nso we can select a subfamily \\(\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal R^{*}\\cup\\mathcal C^{*}\\) consisting of exactly \\(n-k\\) lines (for instance, take all rows in \\(\\mathcal R^{*}\\) and, if necessary, add enough columns from \\(\\mathcal C^{*}\\) to reach the required number).\n\n---\n\n**Step 4 – Verification of the covering property.**  \nConsider any point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)=P_{i}\\) for some \\(i\\),* it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\).\n\n*Otherwise* \\((a,b)\\) is not a reserved point.  \nIf the row \\(R_{a}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal R^{*}\\) then \\(R_{a}\\in\\mathcal F\\) and the point is covered.  \nIf \\(R_{a}\\) was removed (i.e. \\(a=a_{i}\\) for some \\(i\\)), then the column \\(C_{b}\\) cannot have been removed simultaneously, because we removed exactly the columns \\(C_{b_{1}},\\dots ,C_{b_{k}}\\) and the pairs \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) are all distinct. Hence \\(C_{b}\\in\\mathcal C^{*}\\subseteq\\mathcal F\\), and the point is covered by that vertical line.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of the constructed family.\n\nAll lines are distinct: the \\(k\\) sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal or vertical, never coinciding with a sunny line or with each other because they have different equations (different rows or columns). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nLemma 1 shows that a configuration can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 2 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration that attains exactly that number of sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
