) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the family of diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\,\\ell_s : x+y=s \\mid s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are precisely \\(n\\) such lines, because the index set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) has cardinality \\(n\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition of \\(T_n\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1\\), \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSet \\(s:=a+b\\). Then \\(s\\) is an integer satisfying \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), hence the line \\(\\ell_s\\) (the line \\(x+y=s\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\). Moreover \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=s\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\nThus **every** point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \nDefine \\(\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal D\\). Then \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\), all its lines are non\u2011sunny, and the covering condition is fulfilled. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Consequence  \nBecause a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every admissible \\(n\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to the set of feasible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n*The determination of all other possible values of \\(k\\) (i.e. whether configurations with one or more sunny lines exist for a given \\(n\\)) has not been resolved in this work.*"
