y  \n\n\\[\nn+1-b,\\qquad n+1-a,\\qquad s-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence a horizontal or vertical line contains at most \\(n\\) points, while a diagonal contains at most \\(n-1\\) points (the maximum \\(n-1\\) occurs for \\(s=n+1\\)).  \n\nLet \\(h\\), \\(v\\) and \\(d\\) denote the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines among the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines; thus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d = n-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points that these \\(n-1\\) lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nh\\cdot n + v\\cdot n + d\\cdot (n-1) = (h+v)n + d(n-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) we rewrite (6) as  \n\n\\[\n(h+v)n + d(n-1)= (n-1-d)n + d(n-1)= n^{2}-n-d . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the maximal possible coverage by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n^{2}-n-d\\), where \\(d\\ge 0\\).\n\nFor (4) to be achievable we need  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - t \\le n^{2}-n-d . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\le 2\\) and \\(d\\ge 0\\), the strongest inequality arises when \\(t=2\\) and \\(d=0\\); it becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - 2 \\le n^{2}-n . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInequality (9) simplifies to \\(3n\\ge 4\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge 2\\); therefore (9) does not yet give a contradiction.  \nWe sharpen the estimate by observing that **at least one** of the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines must be a diagonal.  \n\nIndeed, if all were horizontal or vertical, the total number of points they could cover would be at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting (4). Hence \\(d\\ge 1\\).\n\nPutting \\(d\\ge 1\\) into (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - 2 \\le n^{2}-n-1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(3n\\ge 5\\), still true.  \nWe iterate the argument: the set \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals, and each of them contains a point of the outermost row or column of the triangle \\(T_n\\). To cover all points of \\(T_n\\) we must use **all** these diagonals; otherw