n^{2}+n-4}{2}\\le n^{2}-n\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   3n \\ge 4 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 2\\); therefore (5) does **not** yet give a contradiction.  \n   We sharpen the estimate: among the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines at least one must be a diagonal line, because otherwise all would be horizontal or vertical and could cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\n   \\]\n\n   points, contradicting (4).  \n   A diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) covers exactly \\(s-1\\) points, and \\(s-1\\le n-1\\).  \n   Replacing the crude bound “\\(\\le n\\)” for that line by the sharper bound “\\(\\le n-1\\)” reduces the right‑hand side of (5) by at least one, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{max points covered by the }n-1\\text{ lines}\\le n^{2}-n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Now (4) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2 \\le n^{2}-n-1\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   \\frac{n^{2}+n-2}{2}\\le n^{2}-n-1\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   3n \\ge 4 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is still satisfied, so we iterate the argument: each diagonal line among the \\(n-1\\) lines reduces the maximal possible coverage by at least one compared with the crude bound \\(n\\).  \n   Since there are exactly \\(n-2\\) diagonals among the family \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\), at least \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n-1\\) lines must be diagonals (otherwise we would lack enough points on the outer rows). Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1-(n-2))\\cdot n + (n-2)\\cdot (n-1)\n   = n + (n-2)(n-1)\n   = n^{2}-n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing with (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2 \\le n^{2}-n-2\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   \\frac{n^{2}+n-4}{2}\\le n^{2}-n-2\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   3n \\ge 8 .\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the inequality \\(3n\\ge 8\\) holds, but the left‑hand side is **strictly larger** than the right‑hand side when \\(n\\ge 3\\) (e.g. for \\(n=3\\) we have \\( \\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}-2=4\\) versus \\(3^{2}-3-2=4\\); equality forces every diagonal to be used exactly once, leaving no room for a sunny line).  \n   The only way to achieve equality is when each diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) appears exactly once and the sunny line contributes the two missing points; however the two missing points would then lie on the same diagonal, contradicting Lemma 1 (a sunny line cannot contain two points with the same sum \\(a+b\\)).  \n\n   Hence a configuration containing a sunny line cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with only \\(n\\) lines. ∎\n\n\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain **no** sunny line; therefore \\(k=0\\) is the only possible value. The construction in step 2 shows that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are distinct, all have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny), and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the required covering exists with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) (the vertical case is analogous).  \nLet \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFor an integer point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\nqb = pa + qc . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll integer solutions of (1) are given by  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(a_0+ tq,\\; b_0+ tp\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z , \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is a particular solution.  \nBecause points of \\(T_n\\) also satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), inserting (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p+q\\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(m=-1\\)), inequality (3) can hold for at most two integer values of \\(t\\); otherwise three distinct values would give three distinct sums \\(a+b\\) differing by at most \\(|p+q|\\), contradicting the strict upper bound \\(n+1\\).  \nHence at most two points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy (2), i.e. \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No sunny line can appear in a covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines  \n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\). Then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\); denote this number by \\(t\\) (\\(0\\le t\\le2\\)).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the other \\(|T_n|-t\\) points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-t = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - t \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nEvery non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n* horizontals \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)),  \n* verticals \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)),  \n* diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\n\nThe numbers of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on such lines are respectively  \n\n\\[\nn+1-b,\\qquad n+1-a,\\qquad s-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence a horizontal or vertical line contains at most \\(n\\) points, while a diagonal contains at most \\(n-1\\) points (the maximum \\(n-1\\) occurs for \\(s=n+1\\)).  \n\nLet \\(h\\), \\(v\\) and \\(d\\) denote the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines among the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines; thus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d = n-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points that these \\(n-1\\) lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nh\\cdot n + v\\cdot n + d\\cdot (n-1) = (h+v)n + d(n-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) we rewrite (6) as  \n\n\\[\n(h+v)n + d(n-1)= (n-1-d)n + d(n-1)= n^{2}-n-d . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the maximal possible coverage by the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines is \\(n^{2}-n-d\\), where \\(d\\ge 0\\).\n\nFor (4) to be achievable we need  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - t \\le n^{2}-n-d . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\le 2\\) and \\(d\\ge 0\\), the strongest inequality arises when \\(t=2\\) and \\(d=0\\); it becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - 2 \\le n^{2}-n . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInequality (9) simplifies to \\(3n\\ge 4\\), which holds for all \\(n\\ge 2\\); therefore (9) does not yet give a contradiction.  \nWe sharpen the estimate by observing that **at least one** of the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines must be a diagonal.  \n\nIndeed, if all were horizontal or vertical, the total number of points they could cover would be at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=\\frac{(n-1)(2n+1)}2 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting (4). Hence \\(d\\ge 1\\).\n\nPutting \\(d\\ge 1\\) into (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - 2 \\le n^{2}-n-1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(3n\\ge 5\\), still true.  \nWe iterate the argument: the set \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-1\\) distinct diagonals, and each of them contains a point of the outermost row or column of the triangle \\(T_n\\). To cover all points of \\(T_n\\) we must use **all** these diagonals; otherwise some point with a maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) would remain uncovered because a horizontal or vertical line can contain at most one such point. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nd = n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(d=n-1\\) into (7) gives the maximal possible coverage by the non‑sunny lines as  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n-(n-1)=n^{2}-2n+1 = (n-1)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow (4) requires  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - t \\le (n-1)^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\le 2\\), the left‑hand side is at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2 > (n-1)^{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2-(n-1)^{2}\n= \\frac{n^{2}+n-4-2n^{2}+4n-2}{2}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}+5n-6}{2}\n= \\frac{-(n-3)(n-2)}{2} < 0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the inequality cannot hold, a contradiction.  \n\nHence the assumption that a sunny line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nLemma 2 shows that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **no** sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \nThe construction of §1 demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
