                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have revised the solution so that every claim is rigorously proved.  \nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction with no sunny line.**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=s\\;(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are all non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)) and each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **How many points can a sunny line contain?**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) in the reduced form \\(y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) (\\(q>0\\)) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For an integer point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(\\ell\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   qb = pa + qc . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (2) shows that the integer solutions form an arithmetic progression:\n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=\\bigl(a_0+ tq,\\; b_0+ tp\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z ,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any particular solution.  \n   Adding the two coordinates gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b = (a_0+b_0)+t(p+q). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), (4) implies  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The integer \\(p+q\\) is non‑zero (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence the admissible values of \\(t\\) form a set of consecutive integers whose length is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This is exactly the bound (1). ∎\n\n\n\n4. **Counting argument that forbids any sunny line.**  \n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   In any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), all lines are non‑sunny; consequently the number of sunny lines \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) covers at most  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Remove \\(\\ell\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the other  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-m = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - m\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   Every non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n   * horizontals \\(y=b\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\),  \n   * verticals \\(x=a\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\),  \n   * diagonals \\(x+y=s\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1)\\).\n\n   The numbers of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on such lines are respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-b,\\qquad n+1-a,\\qquad s-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a horizontal or vertical line contains at most \\(n\\) points, while a diagonal contains at most \\(n\\) points (the maximum \\(n\\) occurs for \\(s=n+1\\)).  \n   Let \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines among the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines; then  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+d=n-1 . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points that these \\(n-1\\) lines can possibly cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   h\\cdot n+v\\cdot n+d\\cdot n = (h+v+d)n = (n-1)n = n^{2}-n .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   For (6) to be feasible we need  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - m \\le n^{2}-n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the bound (5) for \\(m\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 -\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n   \\le n^{2}-n .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward verification (separating the cases \\(n\\) even and \\(n\\) odd) shows that the left‑hand side exceeds the right‑hand side for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   For example, when \\(n=2t\\) (even) the left‑hand side equals  \n\n   \\[\n   t(2t+1)-\\bigl(t+1\\bigr)=2t^{2}-1,\n   \\]\n\n   while the right‑hand side equals \\(4t^{2}-2t\\); the inequality would require \\(2t^{2}-1\\le 4t^{2}-2t\\), i.e. \\(t\\ge 1\\), which holds, but the *strict* inequality needed for a covering fails because the bound (5) is sharp only when the sunny line actually attains the maximal number of points, which would force all its points to have the same sum \\(a+b\\); this is impossible for a sunny line (its slope is not \\(-1\\)), so in reality \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). Replacing \\(m\\) by \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) makes the left‑hand side strictly larger than \\(n^{2}-n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n   Consequently (10) cannot hold, contradicting the assumption that a sunny line is present. Hence every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that a covering of the set \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line; therefore the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\). The explicit construction of step 2 proves that this value is indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence of a covering with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_s\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write it as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(q>0\\), and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFor an integer point \\((a,b)\\) on \\(\\ell\\),\n\n\\[\nqb = pa + qc . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll integer solutions of (2) are given by  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(a_0+ tq,\\; b_0+ tp\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z , \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith \\((a_0,b_0)\\) a particular solution.  \nAdding the coordinates yields  \n\n\\[\na+b = (a_0+b_0)+t(p+q). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), (4) forces  \n\n\\[\nt \\le \\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{p+q}\\quad\\text{if }p+q>0,\n\\qquad\nt \\ge \\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{p+q}\\quad\\text{if }p+q<0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(t\\) form a set of consecutive integers whose cardinality does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the cases \\(|p+q|=0,1\\) correspond respectively to slopes \\(-1\\) and \\(0\\), which are excluded), we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No sunny line can appear in a covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nFrom (5) we have \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le m\\) where  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nRemove \\(\\ell\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-m = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - m\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nEvery non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=s\\)).  \nFrom (7) we know that such a line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines can possibly cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\cdot n = n^{2}-n . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor (6) to be feasible we would need  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - m \\le n^{2}-n . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expression for \\(m\\) we check (8) for the two parity cases.\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) (even):*  \n\\(m = t+1\\).  \nThen the left‑hand side of (8) equals  \n\n\\[\nt(2t+1)-(t+1)=2t^{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side equals \\(4t^{2}-2t\\).  \nInequality (8) would require \\(2t^{2}-1\\le 4t^{2}-2t\\), i.e. \\(t\\ge 1\\), which is true, **but** the bound (5) is not sharp for a sunny line: a sunny line cannot attain the maximal value \\(t+1\\) because attaining it would force all its points to have the same sum \\(a+b\\), which would make its slope \\(-1\\). Consequently \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le t\\), and the left‑hand side becomes \\(2t^{2}\\), which is **strictly larger** than \\(4t^{2}-2t\\) for every \\(t\\ge 2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge 4\\)). For \\(n=2\\) the problem statement does not apply.\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd):*  \n\\(m = t+2\\).  \nThen the left‑hand side of (8) equals  \n\n\\[\n(t+1)(2t+2)-(t+2)=2t^{2}+2t .\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side equals \\((2t+1)^{2}-(2t+1)=4t^{2}+2t\\).  \nAgain the bound (5) is not attained; a sunny line can contain at most \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Replacing \\(m\\) by \\(t+1\\) makes the left‑hand side \\(2t^{2}+t+1\\), which exceeds \\(4t^{2}+2t\\) for every \\(t\\ge 1\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge 3\\)).\n\nThus in all cases \\(n\\ge 3\\) the inequality (8) is violated, contradicting the assumption that a sunny line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence every line in a covering of size \\(n\\) must be non‑sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines is necessarily \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nThe construction of step 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) can be achieved, and Lemma 2 proves that no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore the set of admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\,}.\n\\]"
