gonal \\(x+y=s\\) contains the points \\((a,s-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\); thus it contains exactly \\(s-1\\le n\\) points.  \n\n   Consequently **each** of the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The total number of points that they can possibly cover is therefore at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)\\cdot n = n^{2}-n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For a covering to be possible we need the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\le n^{2}-n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying (5) by \\(2\\) gives \\(n(n-1)\\le 2n^{2}-2n\\), i.e. \\(0\\le n^{2}\\), which is true.  \n   However, inequality (5) must be **strict**, because the bound \\(n\\) for a non‑sunny line is attained only when the line is a diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (or a horizontal/vertical line at the extreme).  \n   In such a case the line would contain the point \\((1,n)\\) (or \\((n,1)\\)), which already lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Since the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are required to be **distinct**, two different lines cannot share the same point of \\(T_n\\) without creating an overlap that reduces the total number of distinct points covered.  \n\n   More concretely, assume the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines achieve the maximal possible coverage \\(n\\) each.  \n   Then each of them must be a diagonal of the form \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s=n+1\\) (the only diagonal containing \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)).  \n   But there is exactly **one** such diagonal, so at most one of the \\(n-1\\) lines can attain the bound \\(n\\).  \n   All the remaining lines cover at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) lines can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n + (n-2)(n-1)=n^{2}-2n+2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (3) with (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\le n^{2}-2n+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality fails for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) (for \\(n=3\\) the left‑hand side equals \\(3\\) while the right‑hand side equals \\(5\\); for larger \\(n\\) the left‑hand side grows quadratically faster than the right‑hand side).  \n   Therefore the assumption that a sunny line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) leads to a contradiction.  \n\n   Consequently every line in a covering of size \\(n\\) must be non‑sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines is necessarily \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that a covering of the set \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line; the explicit construction of step 2 proves that the value \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
