 Assume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   By Lemma 1, \\(\\ell\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Remove \\(\\ell\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   By Lemma 2 each of the remaining lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points, and **at most one** of them can actually cover \\(n\\) points (the only lines attaining the bound \\(n\\) are the three listed in (3), and they are pairwise distinct).  \n   Hence the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) lines can possibly cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n+(n-2)(n-1)=n^{2}-2n+2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Comparing (4) with (5) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\le n^{2}-2n+2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the left‑hand side exceeds the right‑hand side:\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2n+2-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   =\\frac{n^{2}-3n+4}{2}<0\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n   \\]\n   Thus (6) is impossible, contradicting the assumption that a sunny line belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   Therefore every line in a covering of size \\(n\\) must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that a covering of the set \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain a sunny line.  \n   Lemma 2 together with the explicit construction of step 2 proves that the value \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
