h \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   Using these blocks we treat two cases.\n\n   * **Case \\(k=0\\).**  \n     Take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\) and add \\(n-3\\) further lines, each\n     parallel to one of them, chosen so that every interior point \\((a,b)\\) with\n     \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) lies on at least one of the added lines. A simple inductive placement\n     (adding a line parallel to \\(L_x\\) for each new value of \\(a\\) that appears) yields a\n     configuration with no sunny lines.\n\n   * **Case \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n     Start with the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\).  \n     For the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines we proceed as follows.\n     - Choose \\(k-2\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different interior point\n       of \\(S_n\\) (possible because there are at least \\(k-2\\) interior points when\n       \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n     - The still\u2011unused \\(n-k\\) lines are taken parallel to the three forbidden directions\n       in such a way that every point of \\(S_n\\) not already covered by the sunny lines is\n       hit. Because each new parallel line can be made to pass through a whole new row or\n       column of points, a greedy algorithm finishes the covering after at most\n       \\(n-k\\) additional lines.\n\n   In each sub\u2011case the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines\n   is precisely the prescribed \\(k\\), and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line.\n   Hence the construction works for all \\(k\\) listed above.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=1\\) with the explicit constructions for all other\nnon\u2011negative integers yields the final answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The value \\(k=1\\) cannot occur  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly one, say \\(\\ell\\), is sunny.\nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.\nConsequently each of those \\(n-1\\) lines belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_x=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_y=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_d=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\nA line of \\(\\mathcal F_x\\) can contain at most one point of each column\n\\(\\{(a,b):a\\text{ fixed}\\}\\); similarly a line of \\(\\mathcal F_y\\) can contain at most one\npoint of each row, and a line of \\(\\mathcal F_d\\) can contain at most one point of each\ndiagonal \\(a+b=\\text{const}\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the points of the integer lattice inside the right\u2011angled\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  It contains exactly \\(n\\) rows,\n\\(n\\) columns and \\(n\\) diagonals (the diagonals are indexed by the sum \\(a+b\\) ranging\nfrom \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\)).  \n\nIf we use at most \\(n-1\\) lines taken from the three families, then by the pigeonhole\nprinciple at least one of the three families is represented by at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/3\\rfloor\\)\nlines.  Suppose, without loss of generality, that the family \\(\\mathcal F_x\\) is used at\nmost \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/3\\rfloor\\) times.  Hence at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/3\\rfloor\\) distinct\nvalues of the constant \\(c\\) appear among the horizontal lines, and therefore at most\n\\(\\lfloor (n-1)/3\\rfloor\\) rows of \\(S_n\\) are covered by horizontal lines.  The remaining\nrows must be covered by vertical or diagonal lines.  A vertical line can intersect a\ngiven row in at most one point, and a diagonal line can intersect a given row in at most\none point as well.  Consequently each of the remaining rows needs a distinct line,\nwhich would require at least \\(n-\\lfloor (n-1)/3\\rfloor\\) lines in total, contradicting\nthe hypothesis that only \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines are available.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=1\\quad\\text{is impossible.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad L_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad L_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThese three lines already contain all points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=t-1\\); this line\ncovers all points \\((a,t-1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\).  There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such\nvalues of \\(t\\), so after adding these \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines we have used a total of\n\\(n\\) lines, none of which is sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of\nthem.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny ones.\n\n1. **Start with the three non\u2011sunny lines** \\(L_x, L_y, L_d\\) defined above.\n   They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the border of the triangle.\n\n2. **Choose \\(k-2\\) interior points.**  \n   The interior of the triangle contains\n   \\[\n   |S_n|-2n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2n = \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\\ge k-2\n   \\]\n   points (the inequality holds because \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\)).\n   Select distinct interior points \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_{k-2},b_{k-2})\\).\n\n3. **Place a sunny line through each selected interior point.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(m_i\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny, distinct (different slopes or different intercepts),\n   and each contains its designated interior point.\n\n4. **Cover the remaining uncovered points.**  \n   After step 3 the points on the border are already covered, and each interior point\n   \\((a_i,b_i)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S_i\\).  Any point of \\(S_n\\) not yet\n   covered lies on a row or a column that has not been used by a horizontal or vertical\n   line.  We have at our disposal \\(n-(k)\\) further lines (since we have used\n   \\(3+(k-2)=k+1\\) lines so far).  Add these remaining lines as follows:\n\n   *If a row \\(y=r\\) (with \\(2\\le r\\le n\\)) still contains an uncovered point, add the\n   horizontal line \\(y=r\\).  \n   *If a column \\(x=c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) still contains an uncovered point, add the\n   vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n   *If a diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) (with \\(3\\le s\\le n+1\\)) still contains an uncovered point,\n   add the line \\(x+y=s\\).\n\n   Because each added line eliminates at least one previously uncovered row, column or\n   diagonal, after at most \\(n-k\\) such additions all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n   The added lines are all non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines remains exactly\n   \\(k\\).\n\nThus we have produced, for every \\(k\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe value \\(k=1\\) is impossible, while every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and\n\\(k\\neq 1\\) can be achieved.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
