            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and fully rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set that must be covered**  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) we denote the vertical line  \n   \\(V_c:\\;x=c\\); for \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the horizontal line \\(H_c:\\;y=c\\); and for\n   \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the diagonal line \\(D_c:\\;x+y=c\\).\n   Each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each of the three families\n   \\(\\mathcal V=\\{V_c\\},\\;\\mathcal H=\\{H_c\\},\\;\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\}\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (maximal coverage by non‑sunny lines).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(t\\) lines, each parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. *non‑sunny*).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap S_n)\\Bigr|\n   \\le\n   C(t):=\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover the bound is attained by taking the \\(t\\) vertical lines\n   \\(V_{1},\\dots ,V_{t}\\) (or, symmetrically, the \\(t\\) horizontal lines).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) where\n   \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) are respectively the vertical,\n   horizontal and diagonal lines belonging to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   V:=\\{c\\mid V_c\\in\\mathcal V\\},\\qquad\n   H:=\\{c\\mid H_c\\in\\mathcal H\\},\\qquad\n   D:=\\{c\\mid D_c\\in\\mathcal D\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when\n   \\(a\\notin V,\\;b\\notin H,\\;a+b\\notin D\\).  Hence the set of uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   U:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\notin V,\\;b\\notin H,\\;a+b\\notin D\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(|V|=v,\\;|H|=h,\\;|D|=d\\); then \\(v+h+d=t\\).\n   For a fixed pair \\((V,H)\\) the condition “\\(a+b\\notin D\\)” can only\n   increase the size of \\(U\\); therefore, for a given total \\(t\\), the\n   smallest possible \\(|U|\\) (hence the largest possible covered set) is\n   obtained when \\(d=0\\), i.e. when all lines belong to a single family.\n   Consequently we may assume \\(\\mathcal L\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\).\n\n   If \\(\\mathcal L=\\{V_{c_1},\\dots ,V_{c_t}\\}\\) with\n   \\(1\\le c_1<\\dots <c_t\\le n\\), then a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered\n   iff \\(a\\in\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\}\\).  For a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible values of\n   \\(b\\) are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); therefore the number of points covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{t}\\bigl(n+1-c_i\\bigr)\n   =t(n+1)-\\sum_{i=1}^{t}c_i .\n   \\]\n\n   The sum \\(\\sum c_i\\) is minimised by taking the smallest possible\n   abscissae, namely \\(c_i=i\\).  Hence the maximal number of covered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   t(n+1)-\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}{2}=C(t),\n   \\]\n\n   which proves (1).  The same computation works for the horizontal family,\n   and for the diagonal family one obtains the identical expression because\n   the admissible sums run from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (points on a sunny line).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a *sunny* line, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) (the case of a vertical line\n   is excluded by the definition of sunny).  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) then \\(b=ma+q\\).  Distinct points of\n   \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) have distinct abscissae \\(a\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be\n   vertical) and distinct ordinates \\(b\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be\n   horizontal).  Moreover, if two points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) satisfied\n   \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\) then subtracting the two equations\n   \\(b_i=ma_i+q\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m(a_1-a_2)=b_1-b_2=-(a_1-a_2),\n   \\]\n\n   whence \\(m=-1\\), contradicting the sunny hypothesis.  Consequently the\n   three integers \\(a,\\;b,\\;a+b\\) are pairwise distinct for every point of\n   \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\).\n\n   The possible values of the sum \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(S_n\\) are exactly\n   the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are \\(n\\) of them.  Because the\n   sums belonging to points of \\(\\ell\\) are all different, \\(\\ell\\) can\n   contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Assume, for a contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(S_n\\).  Then the set of their sums must be the whole interval\n   \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  In particular there is a point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap\n   S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Since also \\((1,n)\\in S_n\\) has the same sum,\n   the line through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-b}{1-a}= \\frac{n-(n+1-a)}{1-a}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting that\n   \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) cannot contain \\(n\\) points, and\n   (3) follows. ∎\n\n\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (at most one sunny line).**  \n   In any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of\n   \\(S_n\\), the number of sunny lines is at most one.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose, to the contrary, that two distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\)\n   belong to the collection.  Their intersection point \\(P=\\ell_1\\cap\\ell_2\\)\n   is not on any vertical, horizontal or diagonal line; consequently\n   \\(P\\notin S_n\\).\n\n   Remove \\(\\ell_2\\) from the collection; we are left with \\(n-1\\) lines.\n   Any point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered **only** by \\(\\ell_2\\) would now be\n   uncovered.  Because \\(P\\notin S_n\\), the only way a point of \\(S_n\\) can\n   be covered solely by \\(\\ell_2\\) is that it does **not** lie on any of the\n   remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.  Hence after the removal at least one point of\n   \\(S_n\\) becomes uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that the original\n   \\(n\\) lines covered all points.  Therefore at most one sunny line can be\n   present. ∎\n\n\n\n5. **Putting the bounds together.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny\n   lines.  By Lemma 1 the non‑sunny lines cover at most \\(C(t)\\) points of\n   \\(S_n\\); by Lemma 2 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) new points.\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le C(t)+s\\,(n-1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and \\(t=n-s\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le\n   \\frac{(n-s)\\bigl(2n-(n-s)+1\\bigr)}{2}+s\\,(n-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-s)(n+s+1)}{2}+s\\,(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality simplifies to  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\bigl(2n-s-3\\bigr)\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) this holds for every non‑negative \\(s\\); therefore (4)\n   alone does not restrict \\(s\\).  The decisive restriction comes from\n   Lemma 3, which forces  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le 1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can be only \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n6. **Realising the two admissible values.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{1}:x=1,\\;V_{2}:x=2,\\dots ,V_{n}:x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the\n   line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is\n   sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\).  \n   These cover every point of \\(S_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\).  Add a sunny line\n   through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); for\n   instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\; y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to any of the forbidden directions, so it is\n   sunny.  Now every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines,\n   and exactly one line is sunny.\n\nSince both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable and larger values are\nimpossible, the answer is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
