nd every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on the horizontal line.  \nFor the remaining points we use the \\(k\\) sunny lines: each of those lines can be chosen so that it passes through one of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\); because there are exactly \\(\\binom{n-1}{2}\\) such points and we have \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines, we can assign a distinct sunny line to each of the first \\(k\\) of those points.  \nThe uncovered points (if any) all have either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) and are therefore already covered by the two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\nThus we obtain a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); the total number of lines is \\(k+2=n\\) and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the construction yields the complete answer.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\; b\\ge 1,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}\\supseteq S_{n}\\);  \n\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. their direction is different from the\nhorizontal direction, the vertical direction and the direction of the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) satisfies condition\u202f1.\nCall a line *forbidden* if it is parallel to one of the three lines\n\\(y=0\\), \\(x=0\\) or \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is sunny.\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is forbidden.\nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\nEach forbidden direction can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\):\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points because \\(c\\ge 1\\);\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous;\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); intersecting with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives at most \\(n\\) integer points.\n\nConsequently, if at most one line is forbidden, the total number of points of\n\\(S_{n}\\) that can lie on forbidden lines is at most \\(n\\).\nAll remaining points must be covered by sunny lines.\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_{n}\\) with distinct\ncoordinates, because any three points of \\(S_{n}\\) are never collinear unless the\nline is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (a simple check of\nslopes shows this). Hence each sunny line covers at most two points of\n\\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThus the number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\;(\\text{from the single forbidden line})\\;+\\;2\\,(n-1)\\;=\\;3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_{n}|=\\binom{n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>3n-2\\) (indeed,\n\\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-3n+2=\\frac{(n-4)(n-1)}{2}>0\\)).\nHence the assumption that at most one line is forbidden leads to a contradiction.\nTherefore **at least two lines are not sunny**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n1. **Two non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical line \\(L_{v}:x=1\\) and the horizontal line\n   \\(L_{h}:y=1\\).  \n   Both are parallel to forbidden directions, so they are not sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   The set \\(S_{n}\\) contains the points with both coordinates at least \\(2\\):\n   \\[\n  