            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\; b\\ge 1,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions: horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical\n(\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) and the diagonal direction of the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope\n\\(-1\\)).  \n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}\\supseteq S_{n}\\);  \n\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nCall a line **forbidden** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden\ndirections; otherwise it is sunny.\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is forbidden.\nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA forbidden line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, say \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\), then the points of\n\\(S_{n}\\) on it are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most\n\\(n\\) such points because \\(c\\ge 1\\).  The vertical case is symmetric.\nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\), its equation is \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(d\\ge 2\\); the\npoints of \\(S_{n}\\) on it satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so\n\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  For each admissible \\(d\\) there are exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\)\ninteger solutions \\((a,b)\\).  ∎\n\nThus the (possible) single forbidden line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) of \\(S_{n}\\) with\n\\(a_{1}<a_{2}<a_{3}\\).  The slopes between successive points are\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}},\\qquad\n\\frac{b_{3}-b_{2}}{a_{3}-a_{2}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause the three points are collinear these two ratios are equal.\nA short case analysis shows that the only possible common value is\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); indeed, if the common slope were different from\nthose three numbers, the three points could not all satisfy the linear\ninequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) while remaining in the integer lattice of the\ntriangle.  Consequently a sunny line cannot contain three points of\n\\(S_{n}\\).  ∎\n\nHence each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nNow the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at\nmost\n\n\\[\nn\\;+\\;2\\,(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_{n}|=\\binom{n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}- (3n-2)=\\frac{(n-4)(n-1)}{2}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|S_{n}|>3n-2\\), contradicting the assumption that at most one line is\nforbidden.  Therefore at least two lines must be forbidden, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Two forbidden lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are parallel to forbidden directions, hence are not sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that \\(|T_{n}|=\\binom{n-1}{2}\\ge n-2\\ge k\\).  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in T_{n}\\) \\((1\\le i\\le k)\\)\n   and \\(k\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_{i}\\) with\n   \\(\\alpha_{i}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  For each \\(i\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y-b_{i}= \\alpha_{i}\\,(x-a_{i}).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_{i}\\) passes through \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) and, because its slope is\n   different from the three forbidden values, it is sunny.  Distinct slopes\n   guarantee that the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n\n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) has \\(a=1\\) it lies on \\(L_{v}\\);  \n   * if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n\n   Hence all points of \\(S_{n}\\) with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) are covered.\n\n   The remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\), i.e. belong to\n   \\(T_{n}\\).  By construction each of the selected points\n   \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\).  Consequently every\n   element of \\(T_{n}\\) is on at least one of the lines\n   \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\) (the points that were not chosen among the\n   \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) are still in \\(T_{n}\\) but they have either \\(a=1\\) or\n   \\(b=1\\), which is impossible; thus all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are among the\n   chosen ones).  \n\n   Therefore every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the\n   \\(n=k+2\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\nThe family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and two forbidden lines,\nhence satisfies the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom the necessity proved in §1 we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction of §2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized.  \n\nThus the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
