\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nFor each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\text{h}}_{b}:\\;y=b,\\qquad \nL^{\\text{v}}_{a}:\\;x=a,\\qquad \nL^{\\text{d}}_{a+b}:\\;x+y=a+b\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) respectively, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly these three lines.\n\n*Proof.* Immediate from the definitions. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_n=\\{L^{\\text{d}}_{s}\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) diagonal lines, and \\(\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal D_n}L\\supseteq T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L^{\\text{d}}_{a+b}\\in\\mathcal D_n\\). \u220e\n\nThus the set \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) already gives a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) lines covering all required points, and all of its lines have slope \\(-1\\). Consequently **no** line in this configuration is sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 3 (Reduction to any \\(n\\) lines)  \nIf a collection \\(\\mathcal C\\) of lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains more than \\(n\\) lines, then one can delete lines from \\(\\mathcal C\\) until exactly \\(n\\) lines remain while still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Each point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\). Remove any line that is not the **unique** covering line of some point; the removal cannot create an uncovered point. Repeating this operation eliminates all superfluous lines. Since at least one line is needed for each distinct value of \\(a+b\\) (otherwise a point with that sum would be uncovered), at least \\(n\\) lines are necessary, and the process stops precisely when \\(|\\mathcal C|=n\\). \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f3 to the set \\(\\mathcal D_n\\) (which already has size \\(n\\)) shows that a covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines and **zero** sunny lines exists for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\u2014namely the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)\u2014that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains no sunny line (all have slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nHence the integer \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is possible for all }n\\ge 3}\n\\]"
