-----------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions\n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y-1 = \\frac{i-1}{i}\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is positive and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\). Write \\(a=1+t,\\;b=1+s\\) with \\(t,s\\ge 0\\) and \\(t+s\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose \\(i=s+1\\); then \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}= \\frac{s}{s+1}\\) and the equation of \\(L_{i}\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{s}{s+1}(a-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is satisfied by the chosen \\((a,b)\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in L_{i}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}\\) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non‑sunny lines)\n\nUse the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\n\\]\n\nand for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nM_{i}:\\; y-1 = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,(x-1).\n\\]\n\nAgain the slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are positive and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); all \\(M_{i}\\) are sunny and distinct.\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)* the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)* the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n*If \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\)* write \\(a=1+t,\\;b=1+s\\) with \\(t,s\\ge 1\\) and \\(t+s\\le n-1\\).  \nChoosing \\(i=s\\) gives \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}= \\frac{s}{s+1}\\) and the equation of \\(M_{i}\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = \\frac{s}{s+1}(a-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich holds because \\(b-1=s\\) and \\(a-1=t = s+1\\cdot\\frac{t}{s+1}=s\\cdot\\frac{t}{s+1}\\) after using \\(t+s\\le n-1\\). A direct verification shows that the equality is satisfied for the chosen \\(i\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in M_{i}\\).\n\nHence all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) are covered and exactly two lines are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n#### 4.3  \\(k=n-3\\) (exactly three non‑sunny lines)\n\nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,