 one point of \\(V\\) per line. Since \\(|V|=n\\) and we have only \\(n\\) lines altogether, at least one line must be vertical.  \n   – The same argument with the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) exchanged shows that at least one horizontal line is needed.  \n   – All points of \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) of slope \\(-1\\). Any line whose slope is different from \\(-1\\) meets \\(D\\) in at most one point, so to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\) we need at least one line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). ∎  \n\n   Consequently **any admissible family contains at least three non‑sunny lines**, one of each forbidden direction. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-3. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma 2 (no more than three non‑sunny lines are possible).**  \n   The three forbidden directions are distinct; a line can be parallel to at most one of them. Hence an admissible family can contain **at most** three non‑sunny lines. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-3. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (1) and (2) we obtain the *only* possible values  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{\\,n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising the three possibilities.**  \n   All constructions use the point \\(P=(1,1)\\); every sunny line will be taken to pass through \\(P\\).\n\n   *5.1  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,(x-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); thus each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). Write \\(a=1+t,\\;b=1+s\\) with \\(t,s\\ge0\\) and \\(t+s\\le n-1\\).  \n   Choose \\(i=s+1\\); then \\(\\frac{i-1}{i}= \\frac{s}{s+1}\\) and the equation of \\(L_i\\) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   b-1=\\frac{s}{s+1}(a-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which holds because \\(b-1=s\\) and \\(a-1=t\\le s+1\\) and \\(t+s\\le n-1\\) forces \\(t=s\\) or \\(