r \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) (already handled) or \\(a+b\\le n\\). In the latter case we can write \\(a-1 = u,\\;b-1 = v\\) with \\(u,v\\ge1\\) and \\(u+v\\le n-2\\). Choose \\(r=v+1\\) (which lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)). Then  \n\n     \\[\n     b-1=v=\\frac{v+1}{v}\\,u,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(N_{v+1}\\). Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, and exactly three lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=n-3\\).\n\n6. **Exhaustion of possibilities.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces at least three non‑sunny lines; Lemma 2 forces at most three. Hence the number of sunny lines can only be \\(n-3,\\;n-2\\) or \\(n\\). The explicit families constructed in §5 realise each of these three numbers. Therefore no other integer \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }k=n-3,\\;n-2,\\;n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
