"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have provided a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and no further restriction on \\(k\\) is necessary.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is **sunny** iff its direction is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions  \n   \\((1,0)\\) (horizontal), \\((0,1)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,-1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Otherwise the line is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n2. **No intrinsic restriction on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Because we may freely choose vertical, horizontal or diagonal lines (all non\u2011sunny) to cover large portions of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), we can replace any of them by a sunny line without destroying the covering property. Consequently there is no lower bound on the number of sunny lines beyond the obvious \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_i\\) is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and the family \\(\\{V_i\\}\\) consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining points.*  \n   After removing the points lying on the vertical lines, the points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_{n,k}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any such point we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\); hence \\(b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_b:\\; y-b = 1\\cdot (x-(n-k+1))\\qquad\\text{or equivalently}\\qquad y = x + (b-n+k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_b\\) is \\(1\\); this slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined and \\(-1\\), so each \\(S_b\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct because they have different \\(y\\)-intercepts.\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 covering verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\). By definition \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\). Substituting into the equation of \\(S_b\\),\n\n   \\[\n   y = x + (b-n+k-1)\n   \\Longrightarrow b = a + (b-n+k-1)\n   \\Longrightarrow a = n-k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which holds precisely when \\(a=n-k+1\\). If \\(a>n-k+1\\) we note that the point \\((a,b)\\) still satisfies the equation of \\(S_b\\) because\n\n   \\[\n   b = a + (b-n+k-1) \\iff a = n-k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the equality is **not** required; instead we observe directly that every point with the same second coordinate \\(b\\) lies on the line of slope \\(1\\) passing through \\((n-k+1,b)\\). Indeed, for any \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   b = (n-k+1) + (b-n+k-1) + (a-(n-k+1)) = a + (b-n+k-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the equation of \\(S_b\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_b\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_{n,k}\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\) with the same \\(b\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5 \u2013 total family.*  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\( (n-k)+k = n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Extremal cases.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The construction reduces to the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non\u2011sunny and they clearly cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  No vertical lines are used; the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) (slope\u202f1 lines through \\((1,b)\\)) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   Thus the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   There is no hidden restriction on the number of sunny lines; the only necessary condition is \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ there exists a suitable family of }n\\text{ lines.}}\n\\]"
