,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\).*  \nThe diagonal \\(L_s\\) is retained in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_s\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(s=d_i\\) for some \\(i\\).*  \nAll points of \\(L_{d_i}\\) have the form \\((a,d_i-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d_i-1\\).  \nFor \\(a=1\\) we obtain the point \\(P_i\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) by construction.  \nFor any \\(a\\ge2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny-(d_i-1)=\\bigl(d_i-a\\bigr)-(d_i-1)=1-a\\neq 2(a-1)=2\\bigl(x-1\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,d_i-a)\\) does **not** lie on \\(S_i\\).  \nBut such a point also satisfies \\(a\\ge2\\), whence its sum \\(s=d_i\\) is still \\(d_i\\); however the only point of \\(L_{d_i}\\) that is covered after the replacement is \\(P_i\\).  \nConsequently the points with \\(a\\ge2\\) on a removed diagonal would seem uncovered.  \n\nTo avoid this difficulty we modify the selection of the points \\(P_i\\).  \nInstead of the left‑most point we choose, for each \\(i\\), the **right‑most** point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(d_i-1,1).\n\\]\n\nNow the sunny line \\(S_i\\) (still of slope \\(2\\)) is  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-1=2\\bigl(x-(d_i-1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,d_i-a)\\) on the same diagonal satisfies  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = d_i-a-1,\\qquad\n2\\bigl(x-(d_i-1)\\bigr)=2(a-d_i+1)=2a-2d_i+2 .\n\\]\n\nEquality holds only when \\(a=d_i-1\\), i.e. for the chosen point \\(P_i\\).  \nThus the same issue appears; however we can now observe that **every** point of \\(T_n\\) whose sum equals a removed value \\(d_i\\) is either the chosen point \\(P_i\\) (covered by \\(S_i\\)) **or** has \\(a\\ge d_i\\) or \\(b\\ge d_i\\), which is impossible because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=d_i\\le n+1\\). Hence the only points with sum \\(d_i\\) are precisely the points \\((a,d_i-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d_i-1\\); among them the right‑most point \\(P_i\\) is covered, and the remaining points are covered by other diagonals because their sums are different from any removed \\(d_j\\).  \n\nIndeed, if a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=d_i\\) is not the right‑most point, then \\(a\\le d_i-2\\) and consequently \\(b\\ge2\\). Its sum is still \\(d_i\\), but the diagonal with sum \\(d_i\\) has been removed; however such a point also belongs to the diagonal with sum \\(a+b\\) = \\(d_i\\) only, so it would be uncovered. To fix this we proceed differently.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final correct construction\n\nThe difficulty above shows that simply replacing whole diagonals by single sunny lines does not cover the remaining points on those diagonals.  \nWe therefore use a *mixed* family consisting of:\n\n* \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) (these are non‑sunny because they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of slope \\(2\\), passing through one of the still uncovered points.\n\n**Step 1.** The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n**Step 2.** The points that remain uncovered are those with \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nHence the set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\(|U|=1+2+\\dots +k =\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n**Step 3.** Partition \\(U\\) into \\(k\\) groups, the \\(i\\)-th group consisting of the points whose first coordinate equals \\(n-k+i\\):\n\\[\nU_i=\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\},\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nEach group \\(U_i\\) lies on a distinct vertical line, but we will replace that vertical line by a single sunny line that passes through **all** points of \\(U_i\\).  \nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(k+1-i)=2\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is \\(2\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct (they have different intercepts).  \nFor any point \\((n-k+i,b)\\in U_i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny-(k+1-i)=b-(k+1-i),\\qquad\n2\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=2\\bigl((n-k+i)-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the equality \\(b-(k+1-i)=0\\) holds exactly when \\(b=k+1-i\\), i.e. for the **topmost** point of the group. Consequently the line \\(S_i\\) passes through only that topmost point, not through the whole group.  \n\nTo make a single sunny line cover an entire group we instead take a line of slope \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\), which is also sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(k+1-i) = -\\frac12\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((n-k+i,b)\\in U_i\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny-(k+1-i)=b-(k+1-i),\\qquad\n-\\frac12\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the equality holds **iff** \\(b=k+1-i\\), i.e. again only for the topmost point.  \n\nThe above attempts show that a single straight line cannot pass through more than one point of a vertical column unless the points are collinear with the chosen slope, which is impossible because the points in a column have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \n\nTherefore we adopt a different strategy: we keep the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines **and** we add \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which passes through **one** of the still uncovered points.  \nSince there are exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) uncovered points and we are allowed only \\(k\\) additional lines, we must choose the uncovered points wisely so that each sunny line covers as many of them as possible.\n\nObserve that the points of \\(U\\) lie on the lattice triangle with vertices \\((n-k+1,1)\\), \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-k+1,k)\\).  \nTake the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(n-k+i,\\,k+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the points on the hypotenuse of that triangle.  \nAll other points of \\(U\\) lie **below** this hypotenuse.\n\nNow draw through each \\(Q_i\\) the sunny line of slope \\(2\\):\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(k+1-i)=2\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is \\(2\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) we have \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\le k+1-(a-(n-k+1))\\).  \nA straightforward computation shows that such a point lies on the line \\(S_i\\) exactly when \\((a,b)=Q_i\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(U\\) covered by the sunny lines are the \\(k\\) points \\(Q_i\\).\n\nThe remaining points of \\(U\\) are those strictly below the hypotenuse; each of them has first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\) and second coordinate \\(b\\le k-(a-(n-k+1))\\).  \nBut for every such point the sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(n\\); therefore it lies on one of the retained vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) **after a translation**: we replace the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) by the family of lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y = j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k) ,\n\\]\n\nwhich are horizontal (non‑sunny).  \nEach point of \\(U\\) has a second coordinate \\(b\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\), hence belongs to one of the horizontal lines \\(H_b\\).  \n\nThus the final family consists of\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) defined above.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k\\) cannot be negative nor exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\), the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
