oint \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=d_i\\) is not the right\u2011most point, then \\(a\\le d_i-2\\) and consequently \\(b\\ge2\\). Its sum is still \\(d_i\\), but the diagonal with sum \\(d_i\\) has been removed; however such a point also belongs to the diagonal with sum \\(a+b\\) = \\(d_i\\) only, so it would be uncovered. To fix this we proceed differently.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final correct construction\n\nThe difficulty above shows that simply replacing whole diagonals by single sunny lines does not cover the remaining points on those diagonals.  \nWe therefore use a *mixed* family consisting of:\n\n* \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) (these are non\u2011sunny because they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of slope \\(2\\), passing through one of the still uncovered points.\n\n**Step 1.** The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n**Step 2.** The points that remain uncovered are those with \\(a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nHence the set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nand \\(|U|=1+2+\\dots +k =\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n**Step 3.** Partition \\(U\\) into \\(k\\) groups, the \\(i\\)-th group consisting of the points whose first coordinate equals \\(n-k+i\\):\n\\[\nU_i=\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\},\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nEach group \\(U_i\\) lies on a distinct vertical line, but we will replace that vertical line by a single sunny line that passes through **all** points of \\(U_i\\).  \nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(k+1-i)=2\\bigl(x-(n-k+i)\\bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is \\(2\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct (they have different interce