bigr),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is \\(2\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the lines are distinct.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) we have \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\le k+1-(a-(n-k+1))\\).  \nA straightforward computation shows that such a point lies on the line \\(S_i\\) exactly when \\((a,b)=Q_i\\).  \nConsequently the only points of \\(U\\) covered by the sunny lines are the \\(k\\) points \\(Q_i\\).\n\nThe remaining points of \\(U\\) are those strictly below the hypotenuse; each of them has first coordinate \\(a\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\) and second coordinate \\(b\\le k-(a-(n-k+1))\\).  \nBut for every such point the sum \\(a+b\\) is at most \\(n\\); therefore it lies on one of the retained vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) **after a translation**: we replace the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) by the family of lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y = j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k) ,\n\\]\n\nwhich are horizontal (non\u2011sunny).  \nEach point of \\(U\\) has a second coordinate \\(b\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\), hence belongs to one of the horizontal lines \\(H_b\\).  \n\nThus the final family consists of\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) defined above.\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k\\) cannot be negative nor exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\), the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
