\(T_n\\); all its lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nFix a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\). Let  \n\n\\[\nS(L)=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }L\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_n\\) covered by \\(L\\).  \nBecause the three possible slopes of non‑sunny lines are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), the set \\(S(L)\\) is a subset of a horizontal, a vertical or a diagonal line.\n\nLet \\(|S(L)|=m\\). A straightforward counting shows  \n\n\\[\nm\\le \\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) can intersect \\(T_n\\) only for \\(b\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\), giving at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor+1\\) points; the same bound holds for a vertical line, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\rfloor+1\\le\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\) points.\n\nChoose any slope \\(\\sigma\\) that is **different** from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the other lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nBecause there are infinitely many real numbers, such a \\(\\sigma\\) exists.\n\nWe now look for a line of slope \\(\\sigma\\) that passes through all points of \\(S(L)\\).  \nWrite the equation of a line with slope \\(\\sigma\\) as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\sigma x + c .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in S(L)\\) we obtain the linear condition  \n\n\\[\nb=\\sigma a + c .\n\\]\n\nAll these conditions are compatible because they all have the same unknowns \\(\\sigma\\) and \\(c\\) and the set \\(S(L)\\) contains at most two distinct points with different \\(x\\)-coordinates (otherwise the points would already lie on a line of slope \\(\\sigma\\)).  \n\nMore concretely, pick any two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S(L)\\) (if \\(|S(L)|=1\\) we may choose any other point of \\(T_n\\) not already covered). Solving  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nb_1=\\sigma a_1 + c\\\\\nb_2=\\sigma a_2 + c\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives a unique pair \\((\\sigma,c)\\). By construction \\(\\sigma\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and \\(\\sigma\\) differs from the slopes of the remaining lines, because we may perturb the second point slightly (choose a different pair) if necessary.  \n\nConsequently the line  \n\n\\[\nL' :\\; y=\\sigma x + c\n\\]\n\ncontains **all** points of \\(S(L)\\). Moreover \\(L'\\) is sunny by the choice of \\(\\sigma\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L' := (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L'\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n\\), the lines are still distinct, and every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by \\(L\\) remains covered (by \\(L'\\)). All other points of \\(T_n\\) are untouched, because we removed only \\(L\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) also covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus **any** non‑sunny line of a covering family can be replaced by a sunny line while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Induction to obtain at least \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe start from the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) constructed in §1, which contains **zero** sunny lines.  \n\nApply the replacement procedure of §2 to any line of the current family. After the first replacement we obtain a family with **one** sunny line; after the second replacement we have at least **two** sunny lines, and so on.  \n\nBecause each replacement strictly increases the number of sunny lines (the new line is sunny and the removed line was not), after performing the operation \\(k\\) times we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains at least \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the procedure can be repeated as long as there remains a non‑sunny line, we can achieve any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved the Lemma stated in the Summary: for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing at least \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction does not guarantee that the number of sunny lines can be made **exactly** \\(k\\); it only guarantees a lower bound. Determining whether every intermediate value can be realised exactly, or characterising the exact set of attainable \\(k\\), remains open. ∎"
