\dots ,m-1);\n  \\]\n\n* vertical lines  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m-1);\n  \\]\n\n* diagonal lines  \n  \\[\n  D_\\ell:\\; x+y=\\ell\\qquad (\\ell=2,3,\\dots ,m).\n  \\]\n\nThere are \\((m-1)+(m-1)+(m-1)=3m-3\\) lines in total, but many of them coincide\nwhen \\(m\\) is small.  In fact the three families together contain exactly  \n\n\\[\nt:=2m-2\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines: the \\(m-1\\) horizontals, the \\(m-1\\) verticals, and the\n\\(m-1\\) diagonals, the latter three sets being pairwise disjoint because\ntheir slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) respectively.\n\nSince \\(m=n-k\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(m\\ge 2\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\nt=2m-2 = 2(n-k)-2 = n+(n-2k-2)\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nWe will keep only the first \\(n\\) lines among these \\(t\\) distinct\nnon‑sunny lines; the remaining \\(t-n\\) lines (if any) are simply omitted.\nThus we obtain exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines when \\(k=0\\), and fewer than\n\\(n\\) when \\(k>0\\).\n\n**Claim.** The set of all \\(2m-2\\) lines described above already covers every\npoint of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(m=n-k\\ge n-(n-2)=2\\),\n\n\\[\na\\le n\\le m+(k-2)\\le m\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\le n\\le m .\n\\]\n\nHence either \\(a\\le m-1\\) or \\(b\\le m-1\\) (or both).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le m-1\\) then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is present) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n* If \\(b\\le m-1\\) then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nIf both inequalities fail, then \\(a\\ge m\\) and \\(b\\ge m\\); adding gives\n\\(a+b\\ge 2m=n+2k\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), whence\n\\(2m\\le n+1\\).  But \\(2m=2(n-k)\\ge 2(n-(n-2))=4\\), contradicting \\(n\\ge3\\).\nThus the first two cases always occur, and the claim follows. ∎\n\nConsequently the first \\(n\\) lines among the \\(2m-2\\) non‑sunny lines already\ncover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Adding the sunny lines\n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done: we have exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and no sunny\nlines.\n\nAssume now \\(k\\ge 1\\).  From the \\(2m-2\\) non‑sunny lines we keep only\n\\(n-k\\) of them (any \\(n-k\\) distinct ones).  To reach a total of \\(n\\) lines\nwe add \\(k\\) further lines, each chosen with a slope different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance\n\n\\[\nS_r:\\; y = \\alpha_r x \\qquad (r=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nwhere the real numbers \\(\\alpha_r\\) are distinct and satisfy\n\\(\\alpha_r\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  These \\(k\\) lines are sunny by construction,\nand they are distinct from all previously selected non‑sunny lines because\ntheir slopes differ.\n\nSince the \\(n-k\\) retained non‑sunny lines already cover every point of\n\\(T_{n}\\) (the claim above used only the existence of the whole families,\nnot the exact number of lines), the addition of the sunny lines does not\ndestroy the covering property.  Hence we have produced a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that satisfies the\nrequired condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\n*Upper bound:* any admissible configuration must have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n*Realisation:* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited\na concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
